6045585 true] [17592186083247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253469 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083247 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253470 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253400 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253401 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083248 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253402 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083340 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253468 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083350 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253500 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186079865 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186083500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252845 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083500 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y + 5} + \\frac{1}{2y} = \\frac{5}{2y^{2} + 10y}$." 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186083500 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}=\\frac{5}{2{y}^{2}+10y}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083500 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}=\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(y+5,\\,2y,\\,2y(y+5))=2y(y+5).\n$$\n\n- The values $y=0$ and $y=-5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y} & =\\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ 2y(y+5)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{y+5}+\\frac{1}{2y}) & =2y(y+5)\\cdot \\frac{5}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y(y+5)}{y+5}+\\frac{2y(y+5)}{2y} & =\\frac{5\\cdot 2y(y+5)}{2y(y+5)} \\\\ \\frac{6y\\cancel{(y+5)}}{\\cancel{y+5}}+\\frac{\\cancel{2y}(y+5)}{\\cancel{2y}} & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{2y(y+5)}}{\\cancel{2y(y+5)}} \\\\ 6y+(y+5) & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+(y+5) & =5 \\\\ 7y+5 & =5 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $y=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186079877 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186083502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253090 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083502 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{∞}(x)/((1 + x^{2})^{2})dx$." 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186083502 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186083502 1074 "First, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{u}^{-1}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253298 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253307 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087661 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253308 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087662 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253309 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253312 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253313 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253314 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253315 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087663 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253316 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087667 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253367 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087667 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253368 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253413 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087669 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253428 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087669 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253429 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087669 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253430 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087705 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253264 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253262 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087706 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253263 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253317 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253318 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253319 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186087709 1038