17592186045585 false] [17592186097010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253191 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097010 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(-2,2)$ and passes through the point $(6,10).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-2,2),$ we substitute $h=-2$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,10),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=10$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (10-2)^{2} & =4p(6+2) \\\\ 64 & =32p \\\\ p & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(2)(x+2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =8(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=2$ is positive." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094015 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186097012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252960 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097012 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1, 1)$ and passes through the point $(8, 7)$." 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186097012 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,1)$ and passes through the point $(8,7).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097012 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-1,1),$ we substitute $h=-1$ and $k=1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(8,7),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=7$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+1) \\\\ (7-1)^{2} & =4p(8+1) \\\\ 36 & =36p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+1) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =4(1)(x+1) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =4(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094033 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186097015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253025 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097015 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-4,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}=4p(y-4).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-4-4) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})(y-4) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-2(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094039 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186097016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252935 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097016 1074 "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(2,-4),$ and passes through the point $(6,8).$\n\nWe know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,-4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y+4).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(6,8),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(8+4) \\\\ 16 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})(y+4) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094045 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186097017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253238 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097017 1071 "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(3, 0)$ and which passes through the point $(-4, 7)$." 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186097017 1071 "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(3,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-4,7).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097017 1074 "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(3,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-0) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-4,7),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=7$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-4-3)^{2} & =4p(7) \\\\ 49 & =28p \\\\ p & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{7}{4})y \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =7y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094063 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186097020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252846 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097020 1074 "The vertex of a right-opening parabola is the left-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/aa/aaf295d69a75c976df8aaf02ee6680486c502f7ce5719b41682cdf2499cab984.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(-6,0).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094069 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186097021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252990 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097021 1074 "The vertex of an upward-opening parabola is the lowest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/44/44d88798d30ce40741b538e035054ea253d50a59134d0d714bbb1d2af526e493.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(-6,-6).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186094123 13194139533396 17592186045585 false] [17592186097030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253021 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186097030 1071 "Given that $f(x) = \\frac{-2}{4x - 9}$, find $f^{′}(x)$." 13194139533396 17592186045585 false