6253256 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253256 1077 "selection" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253256 1079 #edb/ref 17592186252218 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253256 1080 #uuid "fc56aa86-a6fb-55a8-8dfc-3acc8e9fec8d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251022 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251099 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251335 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186251433 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186252178 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253257 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253257 1077 "selection" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253257 1079 #edb/ref 17592186251099 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253257 1080 #uuid "fd987d2b-51a1-5e48-9b29-2cadc9f301dc" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253041 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253258 1069 "q-116269" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253258 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi }\\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi }.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253258 1072 #uuid "04531fbf-424a-55fe-857b-3e4c5af620eb" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253258 1074 "Note that the factor $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi $ is always negative. We know this because\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⇒\\,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi <-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi $ is always negative, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } & \\ge \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } \\\\ (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2x+3}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } & \\le (\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{2-7x}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)-\\pi } \\\\ 2x+3 & \\le 2-7x \\\\ 9x & \\le -1 \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253158 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253259 1069 "q-116273" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253259 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x-3}\\le 2,\\,x>5.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253259 1072 #uuid "1ee154e6-59f9-5013-ac63-b981a78d5095" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253259 1074 "Since $x>5,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >5 \\\\ x-3 & >5-3 \\\\ x-3 & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $x-3$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>5).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $x-3,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{x-3} & \\le 2 \\\\ (x-3)\\cdot \\frac{x}{x-3} & \\le (x-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\ x & \\le 2x-6 \\\\ -x & \\le -6 \\\\ x & \\ge 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>5$ and $x\\ge 6.$ So, the solution to our inequality is $x\\ge 6.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252870 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253260 1069 "q-116296" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253260 1071 "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[1,5]=[1,2]\\cup [2,5],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3$ | $7$ |" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253260 1072 #uuid "dab1908a-ed96-5f67-a8b2-41493fe7dc98" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253260 1074 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/8f/8fe84e0056eaa8320b4824740a259226b1a18ff1b64ba648d8740d6cac8703fc.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2)+f(5)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1+3}{2}\\cdot (2-1)+\\frac{3+7}{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =2+15 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252895 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253261 1069 "q-116300" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253261 1071 "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-3,2]=[-3,-1]\\cup [-1,0]\\cup [0,2],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $6$ |" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253261 1072 #uuid "6215941f-91c2-5319-8cbc-f79483d60df8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253261 1074 "Let's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/25/25dc419eb5711b0d990909c611a6eacb2754285fb45dc0d969207e63dc09ba12.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-3)+f(-1)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{3\\text{rd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{3}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{2+4}{2}\\cdot (-1-(-3))+\\frac{4+8}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+6+14 \\\\  & =26\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252917 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253262 1069 "q-11634" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253262 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,C=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{3}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{3}$\n- $B={A}^{-1}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253262 1072 #uuid "9db68f61-a1ba-5fec-8a45-32913742b631" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253262 1074 "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is false. As a result, $B\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is true. \nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253220 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253263 1069 "q-11636" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253263 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AC=CA={I}_{3}$\n- $AD=DA={I}_{3}$\n- $D={A}^{-1}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253263 1072 #uuid "3a987f21-4e16-5f44-b8f8-8e69cf93fc24" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253263 1074 "- First, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $CA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CA & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AD=DA={I}_{3}.$ Computing $AD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 7 & -17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 7 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{3}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $D\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253007 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253264 1069 "q-11637" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253264 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,E=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $CD=DC={I}_{2}$\n- $DE=ED={I}_{2}$\n- $D={C}^{-1}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253264 1072 #uuid "297364cd-4444-5a41-ba36-21b226267ecd" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253264 1074 "- First, we check whether $CD=DC={I}_{2}.$ Computing $CD$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CD & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $DC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DC & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true. As a result, $D={C}^{-1}$ and statement III is true too.\n\n- Now, we check whether $DE=ED={I}_{2}.$ Computing $DE$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -2 & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252914 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253265 1069 "q-116407" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253265 1071 "Given the polynomial $P(x)=x(x+3)(x-2),$ find the solution of $P(x)<0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253265 1072 #uuid "29df6030-9c3b-54d6-a595-3cab0e7429db" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253265 1074 "To solve the inequality $P(x)<0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies below the $x$-axis.\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)=x(x+3)(x-2)$ are $x=-3,x=0$ and $x=2,$ which are all simple roots.\n\nAlso, $P(x)$ is a cubic polynomial with a positive leading coefficient. So, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/d8/d87500612fc14636afa38bd01fdc1b90cf7eecd0bfbd546bff7b14d5a5b30fc8.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)<0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is below the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,2).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253118 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253266 1069 "q-116437" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253266 1071 "Given $P(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2},$ solve $P(x)>0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253266 1072 #uuid "93597df0-61c4-5492-be54-c6df20e093b4" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253266 1074 "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}$ are $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=\\pm 2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/89/8916d10a685ad90bdf11c38c0a6e12c8a641a62fd3f0dfde3de644d907b45a8d.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis.\n\nBased on the graph, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253036 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253267 1069 "q-116439" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253267 1071 "Given $P(x)={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2},$ solve $P(x)>0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253267 1072 #uuid "9c3c72b0-d6ae-5dd4-92a0-bb7c3baf5377" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253267 1074 "To solve the inequality $P(x)>0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=P(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+2x-8) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-2x+4x-8) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x-2)+4(x-2)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+4)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial $P(x)={x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}$ are $x=-4,$ $x=0$ and $x=2,$  where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=-4$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](images/a3/a357caad0365f296f87367ee4ac1000f1ea772d0d27650c8340d475ca742828b.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $P(x)>0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253068 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253268 1069 "q-11646" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253268 1071 "Calculate $\\text{det}(L)$, if\n    $L=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253268 1072 #uuid "b53c0c18-d59e-56b1-837f-3b6d41491a5f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253268 1074 "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(L) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =12-(-10) \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252827 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253269 1069 "q-116475" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253269 1071 "If $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x>0,$ determine ${g}^{′}(2).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253269 1072 #uuid "69fa8675-69c9-5de3-ab5d-dc2ec77408a0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253269 1074 "The function ${t}^{2}{e}^{t}$ is continuous on $[0,x],$ where $x>0.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{2}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{′}(2),$ we substitute $x=2$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ={2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{2} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253146 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253270 1069 "q-116501" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253270 1071 "For $x>0,$ find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253270 1072 #uuid "2c64c327-781e-5107-9d50-86331a1fc27f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253270 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{2}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u],$ and so by FTC II we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})\\cdot u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{u} \\\\  & =2x\\cdot {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\sqrt{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252967 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253271 1069 "q-116504" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253271 1071 "Determine ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253271 1072 #uuid "19296d4e-02f5-5e1d-85b0-8bf76523ed9c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253271 1074 "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=3x-5$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of the definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x-5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(3x-5)-1) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252977 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253272 1069 "q-11653" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253272 1071 "If\n    $M=[\\begin{aligned}2 & w \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$\\text{det}(M)=2$, what is the value of $w?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253272 1072 #uuid "605f5d22-0a36-54b2-9fda-393ada9a651b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253272 1074 "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}|=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & w \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-w\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6-4w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nSince $det(M)=2$, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =2 \\\\ 6-4w & =2 \\\\ -4w & =2-6 \\\\ -4w & =-4 \\\\ w & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252953 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253273 1069 "q-116548" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253273 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253273 1072 #uuid "821981f7-de51-5826-a63e-1243bc3ad95a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253273 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and ${3}^{x}+2.$\n\nThe integrand $(6t-7)$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{{3}^{x}+2}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(6x-7)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}(6t-7)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =7-6x+({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6u-7) \\\\  & =7-6x+({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6({3}^{x}+2)-7) \\\\  & =7-6x+({3}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6\\cdot {3}^{x}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u={3}^{x}+2.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =7-6(0)+({3}^{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3))(6\\cdot {3}^{0}+5) \\\\  & =7+11\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252998 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253274 1069 "q-116549" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253274 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253274 1072 #uuid "43ddbcd2-9eb0-5f90-bf0d-9b0ce3927593" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253274 1074 "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{2},\\,b=\\sqrt{25}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253034 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253275 1069 "q-116554" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253275 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253275 1072 #uuid "5db04aa0-901e-5cc9-b6b9-38332e4f57be" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253275 1074 "The standard equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{b}{a}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{16}=4,\\,b=\\sqrt{9}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{3}{4}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253176 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253276 1069 "q-116559" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253276 1071 "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}=1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253276 1072 #uuid "3f8cbfd4-693c-521c-938b-6a82c083d1c9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253276 1074 "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{3},\\,b=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253006 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253277 1069 "q-116590" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253277 1071 "Solve the inequality $(x+1)(x+6)(x-5)(9-x)>0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253277 1072 #uuid "ba6beb67-af4c-51cb-bf9a-f656bee41dad" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253277 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+1)(x+6)(x-5)(9-x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-6,$ $x=-1,$ $x=5$ and $x=9.$ These roots divide the number line into $5$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-6),\\,(-6,-1),\\,(-1,5),\\,(5,9),\\,(9,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,-1)$ | $(-1,5)$ | $(5,9)$ | $(9,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $9-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-6,-1)$ and $(5,9).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-6,-1)\\cup (5,9).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252931 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253278 1069 "q-116595" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253278 1071 "Solve the inequality $(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7)\\ge 0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253278 1072 #uuid "6f270ff5-bc17-53a5-be2e-3042a3d54393" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253278 1074 "First, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+2)^{4}(x+5)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-5),\\,(-5,-2),\\,(-2,7),\\,(7,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,-2)$ | $(-2,7)$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $(x+2)^{4}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-7$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-5)$ and $(7,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup {-2}\\cup [7,\\infty ).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252951 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253279 1069 "q-116604" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253279 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{3}-9x<0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253279 1072 #uuid "f247818d-3412-50bb-b167-aa3d9d409168" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253279 1074 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-9x & =x({x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =x(x+3)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=x(x+3)(x-3).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=3.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-3),\\,(-3,0),\\,(0,3),\\,(3,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253115 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253280 1069 "q-116612" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253280 1071 "Solve the inequality $4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}<0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253280 1072 #uuid "315d73ff-29c5-5311-87e7-72d86c733b74" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253280 1074 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4} & ={x}^{2}(4-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2-x)(2+x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(2-x)(2+x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)<0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=2.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(2,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2+x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is negative.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is negative on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253008 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253281 1069 "q-116614" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253281 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253281 1072 #uuid "a117df1a-bd81-5da6-88f9-198bb5436e1f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253281 1074 "First, we factor the polynomial on the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}+3{x}^{3}-28{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+3x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}+7x-4x-28) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x(x+7)-4(x+7)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x-4)(x+7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-7,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals: \n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-7),\\,(-7,0),\\,(0,4),\\,(4,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,0)$ | $(0,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup {0}\\cup [4,\\infty ).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253088 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253282 1069 "q-116617" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253282 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}-{x}^{3}>16-16x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253282 1072 #uuid "78e7b37d-682c-5e54-b543-522187e2cde5" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253282 1074 "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & >16-16x \\\\ ({x}^{2}-{x}^{3})-(16-16x) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-x)-16(1-x) & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-16)(1-x) & >0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-4)(1-x) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+4)(x-4)(1-x).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)>0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-4,$ $x=1$ and $x=4.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-4),\\,(-4,1),\\,(1,4),\\,(4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,1)$ | $(1,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $1-x$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (1,4).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253022 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253283 1069 "q-116621" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253283 1071 "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $c(t),$ that arrive at a shop counter over a period of $120$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $30$ | $80$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $c(t)\\,(\\text{customers/minute})$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,30]\\cup [30,80]\\cup [80,120],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total number of customers served in two hours." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253283 1072 #uuid "b99eef5b-7f94-5007-bee7-0a63e56c94e2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253283 1074 "In this context, we have $3$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=c(t)$ represents the total number of customers served in $2$ hours.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{c(0)+c(30)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{c(30)+c(80)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}+\\frac{c(80)+c(120)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{10+8}{2}\\cdot (30-0)+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (80-30)+\\frac{6+12}{2}\\cdot (120-80) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 30+7\\cdot 50+9\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =980\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of customers serve approximately is $980$ in $2$ hours." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253197 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253284 1069 "q-116650" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253284 1071 "![](images/4f/4fa141d5af6f23e2eaa1d416def8f84e21e48f775b5b195cbc8ed74e4bb64218.png)\n\nA circular iceberg is melting in such a way that the superficial circle is decreasing its area at a rate of $24\\pi \\,{\\text{km}}^{2}\\text{/year}.$ Calculate the rate at which the circumference of the iceberg is decreasing at the moment when the radius is  $6\\,\\text{km}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253284 1072 #uuid "10207108-a210-580a-9bc1-6306b708682a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253284 1074 "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=6,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=6\\,\\text{km},$ $C=2\\pi (6)=12\\pi \\text{ km}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi $ and $C=12\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{12\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{24\\pi }{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ km/year}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253153 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253285 1069 "q-116695" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253285 1071 "Solve the inequality ${x}^{4}-7{x}^{2}\\ge 7{x}^{2}+5{x}^{3}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253285 1072 #uuid "2abd363e-586c-5631-9577-8af2aadcb659" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253285 1074 "Moving all the terms to the left-hand side and factoring the resulting polynomial, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-7{x}^{2} & \\ge 7{x}^{2}+5{x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{4}-7{x}^{2}-7{x}^{2}-5{x}^{3} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-14{x}^{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-5x-14) & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)(x-7) & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we define the function $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}(x+2)(x-7).\n$$\n\nSo we're now solving the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0.$\n\nThe solutions to the equation $f(x)=0$ are $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=7.$ These roots divide the number line into $4$ subintervals:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-2),\\,(-2,0),\\,(0,7),\\,(7,\\infty )\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(0,7)$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-7$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the inequality $f(x)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to determine where $f(x)$ is positive.\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f(x)$ is positive on the interval $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(7,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, remembering that the roots are also solutions, we conclude that $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all  $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup {0}\\cup [7,\\infty ).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253005 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253286 1069 "q-116773" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253286 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  | $$ | $$ |\n| $2$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253286 1072 #uuid "62324e0e-02ba-5fa3-b9bd-9039e1e1ce56" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253286 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=1,$ we fill $y=1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈3.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253102 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253287 1069 "q-116777" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253287 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3xy,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(2).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253287 1072 #uuid "48d2946b-ee8c-55d1-a161-5a9033aa27d0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253287 1074 "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3xy \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+6 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈8.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252997 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253288 1069 "q-116780" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253288 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-2y,\\,y(2)=4\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(3).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253288 1072 #uuid "db756825-f589-5ab6-839d-d212a79fb1f7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253288 1074 "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-2y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+(-2) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈2.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252996 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253289 1069 "q-116791" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253289 1071 "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}x+2,\\,y(0)=-1.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=0$ to approximate $y(0.5).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253289 1072 #uuid "f8046d33-689d-5dd9-aa1a-4143eae02c4b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253289 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(0)=-1.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)={y}^{2}x+2,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=(-1)^{2}\\cdot 0+2=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(0)$ and ${y}^{′}(0)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(0)+{y}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =-1+(2)(x-0) \\\\  & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(0.5)$ using $L(0.5).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(0.5)≈L(0.5)=2(0.5)-1=0.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253064 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253290 1069 "q-116798" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253290 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(x-5)+4,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253290 1072 #uuid "1d27a8b9-840b-5e81-b68e-56b475bcaf90" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253290 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(0)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(x-5)+4 \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (0-5)+4 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $9$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{1}{3})≈2.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253253 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253291 1069 "q-116800" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253291 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(2)={e}^{2}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{5}{2}).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253291 1072 #uuid "2cb32bd7-bce1-5088-9a7e-4c63d6e35647" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253291 1074 "Since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{5}{2},$ we will use a step size of \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{2}-2=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | ${e}^{2}$ | $4$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | ${e}^{2}+2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(\\frac{5}{2})≈{e}^{2}+2.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252891 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253292 1069 "q-116850" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253292 1071 "Consider the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-y,\\,y(2)=0.\n$$\n\nUse the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ at $x=2$ to approximate $y(3).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253292 1072 #uuid "d74b6457-c511-50d2-ab65-f48342c0f540" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253292 1074 "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(2)=0.$ We also know that ${y}^{′}(x)={x}^{2}-y,$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(2)={2}^{2}-0=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(2)+{y}^{′}(2)(x-2) \\\\  & =0+4(x-2) \\\\  & =4(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $y(3)$ using $L(3).$ We get\n\n$$\ny(3)≈L(3)=4(3-2)=4.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253155 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253293 1069 "q-116876" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253293 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy+1,\\,y(1)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253293 1072 #uuid "1ff5e9cd-1011-59c0-b9d4-fe290fa9e62e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253293 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=-1,$ we fill $y=-1$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+1 \\\\  & =1(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253237 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253294 1069 "q-116878" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253294 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y-7x,\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{6}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253294 1072 #uuid "4bf84dcd-7f32-5b6c-941e-b334619c9e9a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253294 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2y-7x \\\\  & =2(3)-7(1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{6}$ |" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253097 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253295 1069 "q-116886" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253295 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2xy,\\,y(-1)=3\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the cell indicated in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\,?\\,$ | $$ | $$ |" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253295 1072 #uuid "1718b71d-74bc-5780-811c-5db2e873c658" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253295 1074 "First, because the initial condition is $y(-1)=3,$ we fill $y=3$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy \\\\  & =2(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =3+(-3) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $3$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(-\\frac{1}{2})≈0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252857 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253296 1069 "q-116907" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253296 1071 "What is the center and the radius of the circle with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253296 1072 #uuid "39164142-7623-543e-98b1-65a9cd73c9bb" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253296 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ represents a circle that is centered at the origin, with radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =2 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we disregarded the negative solution for the radius since the radius of a circle must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(0,0)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253055 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253297 1069 "q-116927" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253297 1071 "![](images/34/343897550f0f7b9e4a479e47c9191ababa31070dac79dbf4d54befe869661a48.png)\n\nGiven that the points $X$ and $Y$ with coordinates $(-1,2)$ and $(1,-2),$ respectively, are the endpoints of a diameter of a circle centered at the origin, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253297 1072 #uuid "7e38a793-c9a3-5cf1-9d57-1f88f87f48f2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253297 1074 "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{―}{XY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-1-1)^{2}+(2-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253142 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253298 1069 "q-116929" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253298 1071 "![](images/b6/b69a99c42bcdbd7e6ae3434862019b792da6bea498fd92a36595722d4fb66995.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253298 1072 #uuid "1378faa1-06f3-5893-a7e8-8251ca750847" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253298 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-4,-3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=6.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={6}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252863 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253299 1069 "q-116962" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253299 1071 "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253299 1072 #uuid "40b3e760-880b-56a0-a8e4-9d3a2c540a1a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253299 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=-\\frac{4}{5}$ is in the domain of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252842 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253300 1069 "q-116965" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253300 1071 "Which of the following is **not** in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253300 1072 #uuid "43c365c2-ded0-51d1-a6da-24435d19d8af" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253300 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=\\pi -2$ is not in the domain of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253160 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253301 1069 "q-116966" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253301 1071 "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253301 1072 #uuid "084a7b4b-8361-5ab7-b547-58636a48752e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253301 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=-\\frac{7}{10}$ is in the domain of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253163 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253302 1069 "q-116967" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253302 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253302 1072 #uuid "0bd492a5-90b9-5814-b28f-73caeabd307e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253302 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253244 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253303 1069 "q-116970" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253303 1071 "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253303 1072 #uuid "767c1d10-353b-5f6e-af12-3fd3742fad7e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253303 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is not in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253179 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253304 1069 "q-116971" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253304 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253304 1072 #uuid "8e44482f-7e95-5fde-99c9-d20711dd6ffb" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253304 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{3}{4}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252984 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253305 1069 "q-116974" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253305 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253305 1072 #uuid "86245648-d363-527e-bfea-ec2dc9e748ca" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253305 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253042 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253306 1069 "q-117025" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253306 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $(1,\\pi )$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253306 1072 #uuid "a9ed09ea-d30a-5142-9dec-aca74736564e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253306 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(1,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253255 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253307 1069 "q-117077" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253307 1071 "![](images/5e/5e138922d95cbcf6cc6ceba4d7695b110f5414e08f2a5e9e2ae2d71bff70ab5d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253307 1072 #uuid "1568b6f8-f70c-51a1-ba2c-b92acb3124ff" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253307 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(2,-3)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253217 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253308 1069 "q-117081" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253308 1071 "![](images/98/9839540667b095cec69ce6ea429aa99b129e90fee26c4adf1f25ffa6019d5067.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the circle given above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253308 1072 #uuid "5dd64115-dbeb-5cf8-8fc0-3bacf50a0c2c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253308 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-5,0)$ and the radius of the circle is $r=7.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {(x-(-5))}^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={7}^{2} \\\\ (x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253046 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253309 1069 "q-117087" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253309 1071 "What is the center and the radius of the circle with equation $(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=3?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253309 1072 #uuid "e4a072a8-8d88-5c4f-855b-cd6b51714292" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253309 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=5,$ $b=0,$ and $r=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(5,0)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{3}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253193 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253310 1069 "q-117096" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253310 1071 "![](images/16/16d6786a4b1934ed234d4bf0896187aa6e93ec70cf045ddf4b743b1f51eaac2f.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-4,-3)$ and the point $X(-1,0)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253310 1072 #uuid "4be1d8bd-eb38-5bfc-b4eb-dd764bc3beed" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253310 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{(-1-(-4))^{2}+(0-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252975 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253311 1069 "q-117099" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253311 1071 "![](images/2b/2bd7e3cb9594623c6f5dc3fad35c33c71df0efe9e80b2053dde33f4ef9080bda.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-4,-3)$ and the point $X(0,-1)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253311 1072 #uuid "014cf060-ee36-5cab-a46b-0c5421540a60" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253311 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{(0-(-4))^{2}+(-1-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252896 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253312 1069 "q-117102" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253312 1071 "![](images/af/afd2f435a60fbb5a0f0f83c5f8965a74ca107d1ed4b14d03401f7966a323d2f1.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(2,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253312 1072 #uuid "93ab0f69-1e36-5855-80b5-7cee6cddd602" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253312 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(2,4)$ and $Y(2,0),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, we can find the center using the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{2+2}{2},\\frac{4+0}{2})=(2,2)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2$ and center $(a,b)=(2,2),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253057 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253313 1069 "q-117103" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253313 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253313 1071 "![](images/08/08653e0cdc9bb404bd7ef193dfc9f1b5c0884002e83dd72cbcbda5779a2363a1.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253313 1072 #uuid "e7556293-0fd9-5aac-b946-74962289d2c0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253313 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(2,-1)$ and $Y(2,1),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(-1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, we can find the center using the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{2+2}{2},\\frac{-1+1}{2})=(2,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=1$ and center $(a,b)=(2,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={1}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253030 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253314 1069 "q-117105" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253314 1071 "![](images/05/0598fca48db1dbfb0cacadcf2ffb5cd987fc4e922fe85585849763fedac1f28e.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-3,0)$ and $(1,-2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253314 1072 #uuid "7713e957-0cbe-5d3f-93d1-81e2b678b8a3" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253314 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-3,0)$ and $Y(1,-2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-3-1)^{2}+(0-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-3+1}{2},\\frac{0-2}{2})=(-1,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and center $(a,b)=(-1,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253070 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253315 1069 "q-117106" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253315 1071 "![](images/0a/0a0820b21672bbf16d2fdb35d708aefc4129fdc81c7594b55af3bd2ef5b8fc0f.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253315 1072 #uuid "6610e1ab-73a7-5f4a-8c57-8d6e77beff9c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253315 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-2,0)$ and $Y(0,2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-2-0)^{2}+(0-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-2+0}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(-1,1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and center $(a,b)=(-1,1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252875 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253316 1069 "q-117107" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253316 1071 "![](images/7f/7f1573d17cb9a603727b644da6598355875b0be4b8c43a382e562155cb7a095c.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-1,-3)$ and $(3,1)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253316 1072 #uuid "b6f2966b-20a6-5c34-af5a-1e6c31f128aa" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253316 1074 "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-1,-3)$ and $Y(3,1),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-1-3)^{2}+(-3-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-1+3}{2},\\frac{-3+1}{2})=(1,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ and center $(a,b)=(1,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253212 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253317 1069 "q-11717" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253317 1071 "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253317 1072 #uuid "87ca815d-8c8d-5ffa-9415-2a57d9bd87a2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253317 1074 "- For the matrix $H$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(H)=5\\cdot 0-3\\cdot 0=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(H)=0,$ we conclude that $H$ is not invertible. Since $H$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $H$ is singular.\n\n- For the matrix $J$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(J)=2\\cdot 6-3\\cdot 4=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(J)=0,$ we conclude that $J$ is not invertible. Since $J$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $J$ is singular.\n\n- The matrix $K$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\nTherefore, only $H$ and $J$ is singular." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253044 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253318 1069 "q-11719" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253318 1071 "If $F=[\\begin{aligned}3 & t \\\\ t & 3\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $t.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253318 1072 #uuid "18e87006-2d52-52b3-8fdf-cef524aa5a2c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253318 1074 "The matrix $F$ is singular if and only if $det(F)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(F) & =3\\cdot 3-t\\cdot t \\\\  & =9-{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ t & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $t$ are $t=\\pm 3.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253109 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253319 1069 "q-11721" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253319 1071 "If $Q=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ x & x\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253319 1072 #uuid "e6398e19-0166-5ea2-9ff8-a93fab46629b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253319 1074 "The matrix $Q$ is singular if and only if $det(Q)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Q) & =3\\cdot x-x\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =3x-6x \\\\  & =-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $x=0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253162 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253320 1069 "q-117214" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253320 1071 "Which of the following values is **not** in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253320 1072 #uuid "a45be324-5ab0-545f-8f38-6a49681af902" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253320 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d2/d2677563c82c37fd1672a806a18524c97ea5ec17cb4f8ef6d8df23b827ba5e81.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, the value $x=4$ is not in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252963 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253321 1069 "q-117218" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253321 1071 "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253321 1072 #uuid "ba0dabba-17af-5915-b435-1d113593c629" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253321 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The domain of the function is $[-1,1].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $x=\\frac{1}{\\pi }$ is in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253060 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253322 1069 "q-117219" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253322 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253322 1072 #uuid "90cc5fef-b365-52d2-abf2-a118c27ff32a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253322 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $[0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252890 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253323 1069 "q-117221" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253323 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253323 1072 #uuid "1bf0a7be-9fd8-54b8-9e4e-418e2d997e20" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253323 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $[0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $2$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253061 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253324 1069 "q-117223" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253324 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253324 1072 #uuid "b642ef39-9541-573e-bd96-f441cf5d2ac6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253324 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $[0,\\pi ].$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252837 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253325 1069 "q-117250" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253325 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n- $(0,\\pi )$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253325 1072 #uuid "90a74c1f-7bff-59cd-a30f-99c5f38ea504" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253325 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(1,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,0)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\pi )$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253202 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253326 1069 "q-11726" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253326 1071 "If $P=[\\begin{aligned}n & -8 \\\\ -2 & n\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $n.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253326 1072 #uuid "0c7b9ea7-cded-56a3-a2f3-1334f92c0300" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253326 1074 "The matrix $P$ is singular if and only if $det(P)=0.$ Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P) & =n\\cdot n-(-2)\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & ={n}^{2}-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {n}^{2} & =16 \\\\ n & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $n$ are $n=\\pm 4.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252985 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253327 1069 "q-117263" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253327 1071 "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253327 1072 #uuid "58f536cf-e7a9-5282-b794-b38791856a88" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253327 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n\n- There is a zero at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty ,0)$ and positive over $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n\n- $y\\to -\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from above) as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $y\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from below) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The lines $y=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252966 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253328 1069 "q-117267" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253328 1071 "What is the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253328 1072 #uuid "20d40bcd-1bd9-50eb-ae9a-90382cf7ce77" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253328 1074 "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers.\n\n- There is a zero at $x=0.$\n\n- The function is negative over $(-\\infty ,0)$ and positive over $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The function is strictly increasing in its entire domain.\n\n- $y\\to -\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from above) as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $y\\to \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (from below) as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The lines $y=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are horizontal asymptotes of the function.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252952 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253329 1069 "q-117278" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253329 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253329 1072 #uuid "3c2ae5d5-ee50-5ac5-adef-e81c842f8f25" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253329 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-{x}^{3}}^{a}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $-{x}^{3}$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand ${t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{-{x}^{3}}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{t}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t)+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(-3{x}^{2}){u}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{(-{x}^{3})}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-{x}^{3})+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{x}^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}^{3}+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=-{x}^{3}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =3(1)^{14}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{1}^{3}+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252859 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253330 1069 "q-117289" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253330 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(1)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253330 1072 #uuid "59cdd50b-76d3-5bd3-b113-bd6f2cfed6e1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253330 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nThe integrand $\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{u}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}((\\sqrt{x})^{4}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}({x}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}({1}^{2}+3)}-\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252955 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253331 1069 "q-117291" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253331 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253331 1072 #uuid "c930269f-7d7b-579b-a107-b0f3bde563e2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253331 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nThe integrand ${e}^{1-{t}^{2}}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{e}^{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){e}^{1-{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-{x}^{2}}-{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{1-{0}^{2}}-{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =-{e}^{1-0}-0 \\\\  & =-e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253184 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253332 1069 "q-117292" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253332 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(0)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{-{e}^{2x}}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253332 1072 #uuid "6cee9461-ed92-53a9-b4f1-a50a25b92333" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253332 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals, and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-{e}^{2x}}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{-{e}^{2x}}^{a}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{-{e}^{2x}}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $-{e}^{2x}$ and $x.$\n\nThe integrand $\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}$ is continuous for all $t,$ so applying the FTC II gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{-{e}^{2x}}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{-{e}^{2x}}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =-(-2{e}^{2x})\\sqrt[3]{{u}^{3}+1}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\sqrt[3]{(-{e}^{2x})^{3}+1}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\sqrt[3]{1-{e}^{6x}}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=-{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${f}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =2{e}^{2(0)}\\sqrt[3]{1-{e}^{6(0)}}+\\sqrt[3]{{0}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt[3]{1-1}+\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252938 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253333 1069 "q-117326" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253333 1071 "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\pi }$\n- $-3\\pi $\n- $\\frac{13\\pi }{4}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253333 1072 #uuid "c8a8237f-84a1-5d0d-84b6-e4d83283ba2c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253333 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253012 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253334 1069 "q-117327" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253334 1071 "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $\\frac{16\\pi }{3}$\n- $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$\n- $\\frac{1}{100}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253334 1072 #uuid "40693de6-2bd1-57d5-b124-429c993ca637" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253334 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253245 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253335 1069 "q-117328" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253335 1071 "Which of the following values lies in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $-\\frac{e}{2}$\n- $-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n- $50$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253335 1072 #uuid "1cd41a9a-d0a9-5e6c-a58b-1360b3fd4a17" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253335 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The function is defined for all real numbers.\n\nTherefore, all the given values are in the domain of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252816 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253336 1069 "q-117334" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253336 1071 "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253336 1072 #uuid "8386e6c5-c0be-5d00-a9a0-00a17c58167d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253336 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, of the given values, only $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252833 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253337 1069 "q-117335" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253337 1071 "![](images/39/3989bb27499cb1d20740ab347a1205d2c75c85c894829db054906a3bf5eb18ed.png)\n\nFind the area of the triangle $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253337 1072 #uuid "9e9c204a-4096-5552-9af6-ac5a97d622d9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253337 1074 "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =AB \\\\  & =AH+HB \\\\  & =11+5 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is\n\n$$\nh=CH=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\cdot 7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{112}{2} \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252817 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253338 1069 "q-117336" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253338 1071 "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253338 1072 #uuid "9ac39f28-cd79-5e1b-a032-0394adfaac51" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253338 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is not in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252822 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253339 1069 "q-117338" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253339 1071 "Which of the following values is **not** in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253339 1072 #uuid "8315c50b-3e6d-51ba-b8bf-054ce4b2ac93" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253339 1074 "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/d7/d7984b9098d02bfc2b1e9a422aef919533abce54fbb94117e709c7613b0870a3.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the value $y=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is not in the range of the function." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253216 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253340 1069 "q-11735" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253340 1071 "Given that $f(x)={6}^{x}-5{x}^{2}+6x-19$, find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253340 1072 #uuid "50678a5c-afcb-522f-98fb-80e9680afca6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253340 1074 "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({6}^{x}-5{x}^{2}+6x-19) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({6}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(19) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({6}^{x})-5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(19) \\\\  & =ln⁡6\\cdot {6}^{x}-5(2x)+6(1)-0 \\\\  & ={6}^{x}ln⁡6-10x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252885 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253341 1069 "q-117365" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253341 1071 "![](images/43/4310195e821f7c09399073a2b4eb3e10c262ad61cbb3fe8d951645caee51a4d0.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△BCD$ shown above. If the area of $△BCD$ is $42{\\text{ in}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253341 1072 #uuid "e84eca62-cfb1-5ad9-8628-6991aa78d803" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253341 1074 "Let's consider $△BCD.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{CD}$ and that $CD=12\\text{ in}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△BCD$ is $42{\\text{ in}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{CD\\cdot BC}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{12\\cdot BC}{2} \\\\ 84 & =12\\cdot BC \\\\ BC & =\\frac{84}{12} \\\\ BC & =7\\text{ in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot BC}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{2} \\\\  & =17.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253075 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253342 1069 "q-117366" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253342 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253342 1072 #uuid "bfbb710f-9bcf-5482-b898-5f45cf7ceff2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253342 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253128 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253343 1069 "q-117371" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253343 1071 "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3})$\n- $(0,0)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253343 1072 #uuid "d01f8dbc-d72d-57bf-bd1d-25c6d02854e4" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253343 1074 "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{7\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{7\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{7\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(0,0).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253028 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253344 1069 "q-117374" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253344 1071 "![](images/45/45d1ad7a8cf54a09c2605e2afd3af415de4e42726923bf4771af79aa66079f05.png)\n\nFind $AB$ given that the area of $△ABC$ is $140\\,{m}^{2}$ and $CH=14\\,m.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253344 1072 #uuid "cdb5232c-3d46-5fa3-87d6-5f3f651afbaa" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253344 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is $b=AB,$ and the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=CH=14\\,m.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the formula for the area, we can solve for the base as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{b\\cdot 14}{2} \\\\ 280 & =b\\cdot 14 \\\\ b & =\\frac{280}{14} \\\\ b & =20\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $AB=20\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252972 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253345 1069 "q-117408" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253345 1071 "$2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253345 1072 #uuid "dd07643b-ca8f-5e00-9ad5-265bd5d31225" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253345 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =2\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253040 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253346 1069 "q-117411" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253346 1071 "$-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253346 1072 #uuid "6a5d4f00-fa86-576c-9cad-eafafdb18fe3" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253346 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{11\\pi }{6}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253246 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253347 1069 "q-117412" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253347 1071 "$10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253347 1072 #uuid "0fdb4740-bcc2-5647-bec0-b3bf354a5523" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253347 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253204 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253348 1069 "q-117431" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253348 1071 "If the distance between the points $(a+1,a)$ and $(1,-2)$ is minimized, then $a=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253348 1072 #uuid "20281efc-bbe7-50df-b66a-a7f4a2621f43" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253348 1074 "First, let's compute the *squared* distance $S(a)$ between the two points. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(a) & =(a+1-1)^{2}+(a-(-2))^{2} \\\\  & =(a)^{2}+(a+2)^{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+{a}^{2}+4a+4 \\\\  & =2{a}^{2}+4a+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want to minimize $S,$ we compute the derivative ${S}^{′}(a).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =4a+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(a)$ are the points where the derivative ${S}^{′}(a)=0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(a) & =0 \\\\ 4a+4 & =0 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${S}^{″}(-1)=4>0.$ Therefore, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $a=-1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253130 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253349 1069 "q-117441" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253349 1071 "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{3}-1$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=3.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253349 1072 #uuid "09dc5170-6e81-5e4c-903a-ed41666f4efc" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253349 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $1\\in [-1,3]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=3.$\n\n![](images/0d/0d7fcf48509464cdd6c22962b11c3db4fe24a08fa285adeeb9e30114c9667f49.png)\n\nNotice that the region for $x\\in [-1,1]$ lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =-([\\frac{1}{4}-1]-[\\frac{1}{4}+1]) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [1,3],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{81}{4}-3]-[\\frac{1}{4}-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{69}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=2+18=20.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253205 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253350 1069 "q-117442" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253350 1071 "Find the total area of the region between 