e] [17592186253353 1069 "q-117454" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253353 1071 "Find the total area of the region between the curve $y={x}^{5}+{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253353 1072 #uuid "1dd372c9-9e4e-560b-b302-4bd8a1bb83b8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253353 1074 "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}+{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{3}+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=-1$ are our real solutions. Since $-1\\in [-2,0]$, we have two regions: \n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-2$ to $x=-1.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\n![](images/dc/dc57ac72bdc0d496dc9e106c87d9c5f60cd5d8ca11d150f061edda690082cc71.png)\n\nNotice that for $x\\in [-2,-1]$ the region lies completely below the $x$-axis. Hence, the value of\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{5}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwill be negative. Therefore, the area that we want is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}({x}^{5}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-2}^{-1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-[\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6}-\\frac{(-2)^{6}}{6}]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x\\in [-1,0]$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{5}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}{|}_{-1}^{0}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =[0-\\frac{(-1)^{6}}{6}]+[0-\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the total area is\n\n$$\nA={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{49}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{25}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252979 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253354 1069 "q-117456" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253354 1071 "$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253354 1072 #uuid "be230990-e06e-5511-a145-0fc0b52f3652" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253354 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡({45}^{∘})=1.\n$$\n\nSince ${45}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252861 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253355 1069 "q-117457" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253355 1071 "$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253355 1072 #uuid "e9f3c4b9-a238-51ae-b5c9-d1395c8e83c2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253355 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253242 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253356 1069 "q-117458" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253356 1071 "$\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253356 1072 #uuid "beea09e3-0ea6-5329-a924-9df827ecf14e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253356 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by:\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253156 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253357 1069 "q-117459" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253357 1071 "$3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253357 1072 #uuid "3ae088f2-83ab-519c-8adf-de49960f46a7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253357 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) & =3\\cdot ({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253358 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253067 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253358 1069 "q-117461" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253358 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253358 1071 "$-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253358 1072 #uuid "faf88325-d673-5e64-a0a2-e5898606cfcf" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253358 1074 "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3}) & =(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253110 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253359 1069 "q-117462" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253359 1071 "$4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253359 1072 #uuid "24e984a2-585a-5608-b3a2-5f94b69b88d9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253359 1074 "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253360 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252941 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253360 1069 "q-117463" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253360 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253360 1071 "Use calculus to find the closest point to $P(6,7)$ that is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253360 1072 #uuid "b8647f1c-5367-5a91-bc69-e78441ce9412" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253360 1074 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c4fd419e12879af4910e5d8364426c14342c3dadb970d8bbc43d31213a6ff947.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(6,7)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QA)^{2}} & ={{(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12y+36 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-12y+36 \\\\ 10y & =35 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-12x-14(\\frac{7}{2})+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{193}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(6)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=6$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(6,\\frac{7}{2}).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253136 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253361 1069 "q-117482" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253361 1071 "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=16-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253361 1072 #uuid "4ff20307-ce27-5015-aa4b-6179740ecdbd" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253361 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16-{y}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{4} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](images/48/488788313bd32999611a520ec26e4ba6fd68a56e0333a7252a0faaf7107cad90.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=16-{y}^{4}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=-2$ to $y=2.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(16-{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =16\\cdot y{|}_{-2}^{2}-{\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\cdot (2-(-2))-(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =64-\\frac{64}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{256}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253362 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252888 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253362 1069 "q-117485" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253362 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253362 1071 "Find the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=9y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis for $y\\ge 0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253362 1072 #uuid "ad970949-6b8c-5c6c-9a59-687628bd497c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253362 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(9-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region:\n\n![](images/bb/bbcf218e7b6181c861ba4c804357cc53ae6004453914259a9fe98ad5f09ac4d4.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x=9y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ \nTo find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(9y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =9\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{3}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-0)-(\\frac{(3)^{4}}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}-\\frac{81}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253363 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253091 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253363 1069 "q-117487" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253363 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253363 1071 "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $x={y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y$ and the $y$-axis." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253363 1072 #uuid "4c72108d-e895-553c-9eb8-17579eed6bff" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253363 1074 "First, we find $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-6y+9) & =0 \\\\ y(y-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =0,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we draw a sketch of the region.\n\n![](images/75/7509bf6a10838458b110d982740c1399aa36279b9ce4a231f99b76d7c7cee0b3.png)\n\nWe want to find the area between the curve $x={y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y$ and the $y$-axis from  $y=0$ to $y=3.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}({y}^{3}-6{y}^{2}+9y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+9{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{3}-6\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{3}+9\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{4}}{4}-0)-6(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}-0)+9(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{4}-54+\\frac{81}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253364 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252847 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253364 1069 "q-11750" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253364 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253364 1071 "![](images/b2/b2caf9c9ff75af69d9de7bd1409317229b5d34adf2c64464cd2af2229af0476b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253364 1072 #uuid "a9cc90c7-cdf7-5245-a1ee-6180503fa923" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253364 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(8) \\\\ 64 & =32p \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(2)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=2$ is positive." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253365 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252874 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253365 1069 "q-11751" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253365 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253365 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,8).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253365 1072 #uuid "ecb5a0f2-ba13-55c2-8466-28d0f08c1c9a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253365 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,8),$ we can substitute $x=-8$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(-8) \\\\ 64 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-2)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253366 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252815 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253366 1069 "q-11753" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253366 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253366 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,4).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253366 1072 #uuid "52486dc2-a5ad-5323-887e-ec059a62d92e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253366 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(8,4),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(8) \\\\ 16 & =32p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253367 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253187 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253367 1069 "q-117542" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253367 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253367 1071 "![](images/a3/a3a9751a19c9a853c425b81ce63ccb76b4cd4350f152df3dbe00a1cdb0f8cdab.png)\n\nFind the point where the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253367 1072 #uuid "a2e5f12e-d95e-505c-bfdb-3a5f6be1539e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253367 1074 "To find $x$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $y=-3x-11$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-11+1)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(-3x-10)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+100 & =10 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}+60x+90 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $y$-coordinate of the intersection point, we need to substitute $x=-3$ into the equation of the line and compute $y$-value, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x-11 \\\\  & =-3(-3)-11 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line $y=-3x-11$ intersects the circle ${x}^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=10$ at the point $(-3,-2).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252868 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253368 1069 "q-117546" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253368 1071 "![](images/91/91ead412d3e333fd4bfbcce785813a530e66ca39858ca108e9920d349443bb53.png)\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point where the line $-2x+3y=12$ intersects the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=13$ (sketched above)." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253368 1072 #uuid "277bb7fd-8284-514b-90e8-ffc120633d0d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253368 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation $-2x+3y=12,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2x}{3}+4\n$$\n\nTo find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=\\frac{2x}{3}+4$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+4+1)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{(\\frac{2x}{3}+5)}^{2} & =13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{20x}{3}+25 & =13 \\\\ \\frac{13{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{26x}{3}+13 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+6x+9 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x+3 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253369 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253059 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253369 1069 "q-11756" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253369 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253369 1071 "![](images/58/58c92cc542f027ec882191d2b4631f9fcf3704820b2825a18f30dbdc3f59d07b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253369 1072 #uuid "5b52b18c-f18d-51d2-9931-b8f3ebe75944" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253369 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-4,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,2),$ we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 4 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253370 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252954 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253370 1069 "q-117569" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253370 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253370 1071 "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{4})?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253370 1072 #uuid "3e84da28-f1c2-5f42-8b28-8c5abbfd888a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253370 1074 "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$ \n\nSince $A=1,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|1|=1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252932 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253371 1069 "q-11757" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253371 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-6,-4).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253371 1072 #uuid "2ad97703-d400-5d43-94f9-d8eae5764f59" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253371 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-6,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 16 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{2}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{8}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253372 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253198 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253372 1069 "q-117570" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253372 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253372 1071 "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+3)?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253372 1072 #uuid "1489edd6-f3f4-5ab1-9410-746b5fd6547f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253372 1074 "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=4,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|4|=4.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253373 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253079 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253373 1069 "q-117574" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253373 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253373 1071 "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+1)?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253373 1072 #uuid "7867732e-c747-51f4-a678-9910706e6d84" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253373 1074 "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=-7,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|-7|=7.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253374 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252901 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253374 1069 "q-117576" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253374 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253374 1071 "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{5}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253374 1072 #uuid "86dec134-c288-5f12-9971-6b0570d7076d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253374 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{5}=-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $A=-\\frac{9}{5}.$ Therefore, the amplitude is $|A|=|-\\frac{9}{5}|=\\frac{9}{5}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253375 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253063 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253375 1069 "q-117578" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253375 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253375 1071 "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253375 1072 #uuid "53685d44-d788-5019-bc63-97c441bd2453" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253375 1074 "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{3}{4})}=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253376 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253141 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253376 1069 "q-117579" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253376 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253376 1071 "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4x}{3}-{45}^{∘})?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253376 1072 #uuid "e0d5cec2-04d5-542c-903e-28cdf3f5b313" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253376 1074 "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{(\\frac{4}{3})}={270}^{∘}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253377 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252852 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253377 1069 "q-117598" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253377 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253377 1071 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+{30}^{∘})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253377 1072 #uuid "077801ea-8a82-5ade-8707-1a45b4b6b296" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253377 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=6x+{30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 6x+{30}^{∘} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 6x & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-{5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=-{5}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘},$ where $n$ is any integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253378 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253121 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253378 1069 "q-117602" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253378 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253378 1071 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253378 1072 #uuid "e6c40bb9-f96b-5776-9878-956cda7f4ab8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253378 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is any integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253379 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252860 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253379 1069 "q-117604" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253379 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253379 1071 "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253379 1072 #uuid "07bc26ba-e0fc-5df0-aee6-46276118de95" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253379 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is any integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253380 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253249 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253380 1069 "q-117609" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253380 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253380 1072 #uuid "21457735-cc06-5dc4-85da-719f77cfb1e9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253380 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 5x & =-\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5},$ where $n$ is any integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253381 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252908 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253381 1069 "q-117612" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253381 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253381 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253381 1072 #uuid "ad397087-e235-5fd2-b060-371e8c5fcb6f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253381 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =3\\pi +6n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=3\\pi +6n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252946 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253382 1069 "q-117613" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253382 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its maximum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253382 1072 #uuid "f82af0ab-61c0-554b-921b-23d086807960" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253382 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the maxima of\n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe $X$-values at the maxima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the maxima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =2n\\pi  \\\\ 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =2n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that maximize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is any integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253383 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252843 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253383 1069 "q-117618" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253383 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253383 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253383 1072 #uuid "1b5bbefb-2661-5ebb-a901-2b55b10c8d0b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253383 1074 "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=(2n+1)\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =(2n+1)\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =2n\\pi +\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+8n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253384 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253195 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253384 1069 "q-11762" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253384 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253384 1071 "![](images/a2/a2f289efe5b8f44c27b5d1ffe6d46d58a658f7f81155736288bc621909dd7727.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253384 1072 #uuid "42337ba6-a7cb-5434-9483-5084bb012a1a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253384 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-6,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-6,6),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 36 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253385 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253095 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253385 1069 "q-117633" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253385 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253385 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}=\\frac{1}{{w}^{2}-5w}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253385 1072 #uuid "456d74c0-0d84-59eb-8b19-aaac9fb01d5b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253385 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}=\\frac{1}{w(w-5)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(w-5,\\,3w,\\,w(w-5))=3w(w-5).\n$$\n\n- The values $w=0$ and $w=5$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}=\\frac{1}{w(w-5)} \\\\ 3w(w-5)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{w-5}-\\frac{1}{3w}) & =3w(w-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{w(w-5)} \\\\ \\frac{6w(w-5)}{w-5}-\\frac{3w(w-5)}{3w}=\\frac{3w(w-5)}{w(w-5)} \\\\ \\frac{6w\\cancel{(w-5)}}{\\cancel{w-5}}-\\frac{\\cancel{3w}(w-5)}{\\cancel{3w}}=\\frac{3\\cancel{w(w-5)}}{\\cancel{w(w-5)}} \\\\ 6w-(w-5) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $w,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6w-(w-5) & =3 \\\\ 5w+5 & =3 \\\\ 5w & =-2 \\\\ w & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 0$ and $-\\frac{2}{5}\\ne 5,$ we conclude that $w=-\\frac{2}{5}$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $w=-\\frac{2}{5}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252919 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253386 1069 "q-117634" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253386 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V}=\\frac{2}{2{V}^{2}+V}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253386 1072 #uuid "7b13a932-fbde-51f3-817a-80b8a854ae44" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253386 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V}=\\frac{2}{V(2V+1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2V+1,\\,V,\\,V(2V+1))=V(2V+1).\n$$\n\n- The values $V=0$ and $V=-\\frac{1}{2}$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V} & =\\frac{2}{V(2V+1)} \\\\ V(2V+1)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{2V+1}-\\frac{1}{V}) & =V(2V+1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{V(2V+1)} \\\\ \\frac{2V(2V+1)}{2V+1}-\\frac{V(2V+1)}{V} & =\\frac{2V(2V+1)}{V(2V+1)} \\\\ \\frac{2V\\cancel{(2V+1)}}{\\cancel{2V+1}}-\\frac{\\cancel{V}(2V+1)}{\\cancel{V}} & =\\frac{2\\cancel{V(2V+1)}}{\\cancel{V(2V+1)}} \\\\ 2V-(2V+1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2V-(2V+1) & =2 \\\\ 2V-2V-1 & =2 \\\\ -1 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a false statement. Therefore, we conclude that the system has no solutions." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253387 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252849 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253387 1069 "q-117637" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253387 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253387 1071 "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$, the $y$-axis, and lines  $y=1$ and $y=3.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253387 1072 #uuid "58d5c577-c726-5c8e-a1f0-5cd965b1b3d8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253387 1074 "First, we make $x$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ {e}^{y} & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{3}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{e}^{y}{|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2({e}^{3}-{e}^{1}) \\\\  & =2({e}^{3}-e)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253388 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253074 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253388 1069 "q-11764" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253388 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253388 1071 "![](images/83/83439b98fff25c021a2713448779167f25a0b9f04dd0d14b115a55f915e3501d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253388 1072 #uuid "547a5328-8c82-5389-8e2c-39909fcd12b1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253388 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(12,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(12,6),$ we can substitute $x=12$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(12) \\\\ 36 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253389 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252821 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253389 1069 "q-117658" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253389 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9x+2\\pi }{3})+4.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253389 1072 #uuid "50009c8b-7a58-5669-927f-169a6eff91b4" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253389 1074 "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{9x+2\\pi }{3})+4=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=3,$ and so the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253390 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253113 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253390 1069 "q-117659" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253390 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253390 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)+6.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253390 1072 #uuid "7daa9d90-3598-5b04-bfa2-8fabe3b59b76" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253390 1074 "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=3.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253391 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253137 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253391 1069 "q-11766" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253391 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253391 1071 "![](images/67/6723e1c76d63216151e7c78d67c882ae98f9af950675e37ced726e8ce5aea4d3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253391 1072 #uuid "9a5543e1-6bbc-55cb-a040-aa25b0325f8e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253391 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-12,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-12,8),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{4}{3}$ is negative." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253392 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252981 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253392 1069 "q-117661" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253392 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253392 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253392 1072 #uuid "dc8f3fd2-a7ad-59ae-81ee-947a33e17f52" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253392 1074 "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4})=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=2,$ and so the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253099 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253393 1069 "q-117662" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253393 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x+5, & x<3 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+13, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253393 1072 #uuid "edabc5ea-7962-5769-b1ba-f1b3b2333238" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253393 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{9}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{9}(\\frac{1}{3}x+13)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+5x){|}_{-1}^{3}+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+13x){|}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =[3\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2}+5(3)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-5(-1)]+[\\frac{{9}^{2}}{6}+13(9)-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{6}-13(3)] \\\\  & =[12+20]+[12+78] \\\\  & =32+90 \\\\  & =122.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252922 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253394 1069 "q-117666" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253394 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}7-3x, & x<-1 \\\\ 10, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253394 1072 #uuid "444a864a-4329-515b-a5d7-ab51253bb56b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253394 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-5}^{-1}(7-3x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}10\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(7x-3\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}){|}_{-5}^{-1}+10x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =[7(-1)-3\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{2}-7(-5)+3\\frac{(-5)^{2}}{2}]+10[(1)-(-1)] \\\\  & =[28+36]+10[2] \\\\  & =64+20 \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253209 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253395 1069 "q-117669" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253395 1071 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}, & x<0 \\\\ 2x+1, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253395 1072 #uuid "54276c2e-46f0-5bfc-a206-23daf7d5a1e7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253395 1074 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{-2}^{0}+({x}^{2}+x){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[(0)-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}-(-2)+\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}]+[(2)^{2}+2-(0)^{2}-(0)] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}+6 \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252810 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253396 1069 "q-11767" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253396 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-4,-6).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253396 1072 #uuid "aefac8ce-e3dc-5a02-a1b4-1b585e22a2ae" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253396 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,-6),$ we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-6)^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 36 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-9x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253397 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253011 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253397 1069 "q-117670" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253397 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253397 1071 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253397 1072 #uuid "09fd422a-244d-51a8-a7b3-4942257b716a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253397 1074 "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =n\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =4n\\pi +\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253398 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253206 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253398 1069 "q-117671" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253398 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253398 1071 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the zeros of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+\\pi )?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253398 1072 #uuid "b815f475-45da-5a0b-9cd2-7eb23bea5a6e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253398 1074 "The zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+\\pi ),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x+\\pi ).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x+\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253399 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253180 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253399 1069 "q-117680" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253399 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253399 1071 "![](images/a4/a433e9e9f2018c1f964f13b0ef248f72253f99f071f44e029c928598e9d04a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253399 1072 #uuid "485c2fdf-7dde-569e-8977-e58d8f5860bf" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253399 1074 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253400 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252830 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253400 1069 "q-117682" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253400 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253400 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{{V}^{2}-16}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253400 1072 #uuid "568a0ae1-5a0c-5cc0-9462-af7e9086fc72" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253400 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}=\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(V+4),\\,V-4,\\,(V+4)(V-4))=2(V+4)(V-4).\n$$\n\n- The values $V=-4$ and $V=4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4} & =\\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ 2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{1}{V-4}) & =2(V+4)(V-4)\\cdot \\frac{8}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{2(V+4)}+\\frac{2(V+4)(V-4)}{V-4} & =\\frac{16(V+4)(V-4)}{(V+4)(V-4)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2(V+4)}(V-4)}{\\cancel{2(V+4)}}+\\frac{2(V+4)\\cancel{(V-4)}}{\\cancel{V-4}} & =\\frac{16\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}}{\\cancel{(V+4)(V-4)}} \\\\ (V-4)+2(V+4) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(V-4)+2(V+4) & =16 \\\\ V-4+2V+8 & =16 \\\\ 3V & =12 \\\\ V & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $V=4$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253401 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253227 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253401 1069 "q-117687" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253401 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253401 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{{m}^{2}+m-12}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253401 1072 #uuid "237cb996-b5fc-51c0-b848-df3a338c9048" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253401 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}=\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m+4,\\,m-3,\\,(m+4)(m-3))=(m+4)(m-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $m=-4$ and $m=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3} & =\\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ (m+4)(m-3)\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m+4}+\\frac{1}{m-3}) & =(m+4)(m-3)\\cdot \\frac{3}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{m+4}+\\frac{(m+4)(m-3)}{m-3} & =\\frac{3(m+4)(m-3)}{(m+4)(m-3)} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)}(m-3)}{\\cancel{m+4}}+\\frac{(m+4)\\cancel{(m-3)}}{\\cancel{m-3}} & =\\frac{3\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}}{\\cancel{(m+4)(m-3)}} \\\\ 3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(m-3)+(m+4) & =3 \\\\ 4m-5 & =3 \\\\ 4m & =8 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $2\\ne -4$ and $2\\ne 3,$ we conclude that $m=2$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $m=2.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253402 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253125 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253402 1069 "q-117688" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253402 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253402 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{2(s+4)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+3s-4}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253402 1072 #uuid "bbbd0b33-6531-5ae7-87a7-0cd0379d3a7f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253402 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2(s+4)}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{(s+4)(s-1)}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2(s+4),\\,s-1,\\,(s+4)(s-1)))=2(s+4)(s-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $s=-4$ and $s=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2(s+4)} & =\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{(s+4)(s-1)} \\\\ 2(s+4)(s-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2(s+4)} & =2(s+4)(s-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{3}{(s+4)(s-1)}) \\\\ \\frac{2(s+4)(s-1)}{2(s+4)} & =\\frac{2(s+4)(s-1)}{s-1}-\\frac{6(s+4)(s-1)}{(s+4)(s-1)} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2(s+4)}(s-1)}{\\cancel{2(s+4)}} & =\\frac{2(s+4)\\cancel{(s-1)}}{\\cancel{s-1}}-\\frac{6\\cancel{(s+4)(s-1)}}{\\cancel{(s+4)(s-1)}} \\\\ (s-1) & =2(s+4)-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-1) & =2(s+4)-6 \\\\ s-1 & =2s+2 \\\\ s & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-3\\ne -4$ and $-3\\ne 1,$ we conclude that $s=-3$ is a valid solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution to our original equation is $s=-3.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253124 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253403 1069 "q-117692" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253403 1071 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function  $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-5,$  where \n$x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253403 1072 #uuid "41c041cc-5fd3-5771-8502-8b1349fc86e0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253403 1074 "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-5,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & =n\\cdot {60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=n\\cdot {60}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253086 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253404 1069 "q-117698" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253404 1071 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253404 1072 #uuid "1a30a0cb-a192-58f4-b341-cb115dd8b12f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253404 1074 "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252862 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253405 1069 "q-11770" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253405 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(5,5).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253405 1072 #uuid "e462cce5-27ce-5a33-a1f1-f95fb8cf5eed" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253405 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(5,5),$ we can substitute $x=5$ and $y=5$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {5}^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 25 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252887 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253406 1069 "q-117700" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253406 1071 "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function  $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253406 1072 #uuid "5047cf25-b739-562d-83c1-100604cd7d8b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253406 1074 "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\pi x}{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\pi x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}+3n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}+3n,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253256 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253407 1069 "q-117702" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253407 1071 "![](images/f7/f725b571954587b7b88426a254d7a6fc05f09b63ab2cc94b81303a510e8c1798.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253407 1072 #uuid "d647eaf0-9519-521e-a229-1bff754ddbe0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253407 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $3$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0],$ the line segment for $x\\in [0,2),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [2,3].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [2,3].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =bh \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-2+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253226 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253408 1069 "q-117706" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253408 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253408 1072 #uuid "9df0ed13-fe26-5647-9ee9-26d80317a291" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253408 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ 4x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{3\\cdot 4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253409 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252850 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253409 1069 "q-117707" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253409 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253409 1071 "What is the domain of the function  $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253409 1072 #uuid "9781ee8c-a2f6-5b72-adf5-6e3e834a8a1e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253409 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =2\\pi +4n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne 2\\pi +4n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253410 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252884 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253410 1069 "q-117708" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253410 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253410 1071 "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x)+2?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253410 1072 #uuid "832f28be-4934-518e-a4c9-ca6da407ada4" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253410 1074 "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\nx\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x)+2,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(5x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n5x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2\\cdot 5}+\\frac{n\\pi }{5} \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{n\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{n\\pi }{5},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253411 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252971 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253411 1069 "q-117712" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253411 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253411 1071 "![](images/45/45e506d3b6d3a2ca1a9b16a061f5c081e10b8c578796d7d4a1dfab6e6f793520.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253411 1072 #uuid "b8467112-b8ce-58b8-acef-c1b741ea25db" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253411 1074 "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-1,1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-4.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =2-2+4-4.5 \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253412 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253230 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253412 1069 "q-11772" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253412 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253412 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-7,-7).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253412 1072 #uuid "4e14f2af-b637-53ee-90cb-4edef45fef4f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253412 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-7,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-7$ and $y=-7$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-7)^{2} & =4p(-7) \\\\ 49 & =-28p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{7}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253413 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252835 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253413 1069 "q-117734" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253413 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253413 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points where the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ intersects the line $y=-3x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253413 1072 #uuid "9c293046-5350-5417-bbd1-597cbb9ecd24" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253413 1074 "To find $x$-coordinates of the intersection points, we need to substitute $y=-3x$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(-3x+1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+9{x}^{2}-6x+1 & =1 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-4x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we compute the discriminant $D$ of the above quadratic equation, then we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 10\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =16-40 \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=-24<0$, there are no real solutions to the equation $10{x}^{2}-4x+1=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the circle $(x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}=1$ does not intersect the line $y=-3x.$ \n\nThe corresponding diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/87/87f75953dbe795a8e5db201778b4ff3127f02bae30ef3b8e8ffa7bd7956c1bc3.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253414 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252970 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253414 1069 "q-117749" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253414 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253414 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $y=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253414 1072 #uuid "fd2cfbb0-58d5-5839-b3b9-0140d20d0785" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253414 1074 "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{csc}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=6.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253415 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253103 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253415 1069 "q-117751" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253415 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253415 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $y=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253415 1072 #uuid "dfe6b278-448e-5b3e-ae3a-4c20840dfd3e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253415 1074 "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{sec}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=4\\pi .$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252883 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253416 1069 "q-117752" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253416 1071 "Calculate the period of the function $y=-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253416 1072 #uuid "b44fb065-7fc5-53f5-aa2b-171c7b74a7ad" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253416 1074 "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{csc}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{6})}=12\\pi .$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252964 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253417 1069 "q-11779" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253417 1071 "![](images/b0/b09f63373bba1bb796f1832a4c7aa33ec220186e75bb84ccc79dcc46b662636e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253417 1072 #uuid "4844153a-cf84-57ba-8cb5-907264c75fda" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253417 1074 "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-12).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-12),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-12$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253168 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253418 1069 "q-117795" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253418 1071 "![](images/0f/0f920d90aa215300e720ace10bfec9292440d20f32c9b907854b984014f77427.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253418 1072 #uuid "7f5a48a1-5998-564e-8067-a60e891e1877" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253418 1074 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+(-3)=0.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)-3.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253181 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253419 1069 "q-117798" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253419 1071 "![](images/45/454c2746d17f6444a9fa5762baae3927fb72eff6a7e51a1df38bc4f325a4a830.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253419 1072 #uuid "b0fa25dc-6bee-5930-931e-86bd1acea862" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253419 1074 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $2$ units up. Hence, $D=2.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+2=\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})+2.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253223 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253420 1069 "q-117800" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253420 1071 "![](images/60/6003a85e0038b7e6562381cd338a7597877d1152adbeb476b7141eee74ba06ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253420 1072 #uuid "15fef2c4-3c6f-5cf8-99dc-a49b56e65469" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253420 1074 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{5}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253082 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253421 1069 "q-117803" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253421 1071 "What is the value of $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2$ at its local maximum points?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253421 1072 #uuid "12dce0a0-75bf-584e-b4ec-b1bfde99680c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253421 1074 "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$ are **local minimums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$  For now, we're interested in the local minimums (not local maximums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -3 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -3+2 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-1$ at its local maximum points." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252892 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253422 1069 "q-117804" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253422 1071 "What is the value of $f(x)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3$ at its local minimum points?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253422 1072 #uuid "334a5b82-93d0-57d2-8586-f1e8ca26194d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253422 1074 "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-\\frac{2}{3}$ and adding $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\ge \\frac{2}{3}+3 \\\\  & -\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+3\\ge \\frac{11}{3} \\\\  & f(x)\\ge \\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\frac{11}{3}$ at its local minimum points." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253073 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253423 1069 "q-117805" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253423 1071 "What is the value of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1$ at its local maximum points?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253423 1072 #uuid "97fbbd90-72d5-5259-afd8-7a25639443c6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253423 1074 "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $4$ and adding $1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -4 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\le -4+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-3$ at the local maximum points." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252945 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253424 1069 "q-117807" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253424 1071 "What is the value of $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)-5$ at its local minimum points?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253424 1072 #uuid "2ce6c454-173a-5003-9b4b-6cf0116748da" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253424 1074 "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and subtracting $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\le -1 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\ge 3 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)-5\\ge 3-5 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)-5\\ge -2 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-2$ at its local minimum points." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252992 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253425 1069 "q-117809" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253425 1071 "Which expression gives the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253425 1072 #uuid "b0fbfbab-de05-59b4-b74a-867ddf558108" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253425 1074 "The vertical asymptotes of the function $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the vertical asymptotes of $2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘}=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{4}-{60}^{∘} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =4\\cdot {60}^{∘}+4\\cdot n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x={240}^{∘}+n\\cdot {720}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253143 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253426 1069 "q-11781" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253426 1071 "![](images/e5/e588a9250583251ba2fd85b789eb3a856970fb6e7044289257a4ed97e3bbf1a0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253426 1072 #uuid "73e0be4b-b8c0-5d2c-baec-36cb996c3031" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253426 1074 "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253189 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253427 1069 "q-11782" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253427 1071 "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(4,-6).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253427 1072 #uuid "15283c46-c4af-5a49-a06f-7a6077e8f4df" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253427 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 16 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{2}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{8}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252921 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253428 1069 "q-117851" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253428 1071 "![](images/0e/0e5d6c9ce3198b5950c8a5396cf5500f4bddd232fbe44613611873c294dda6ea.png)\n\nThe line $3y+x=12$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253428 1072 #uuid "b2ced06e-491e-5c02-9d8a-f6fcb8e39a2a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253428 1074 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+x & =12 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-0 & =12 \\\\ 3y & =12 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(12,0)$ and $(0,4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{12+0}{2},\\frac{0+4}{2})=(6,2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(6,2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(12,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(6-12)}^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-6)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(6,2)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{10},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-6)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ (x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253429 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252819 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253429 1069 "q-117857" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253429 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253429 1071 "![](images/51/51d811f0b628a1854542d85954c26ca4658602cfcf7c207fbcfdb41115933139.png)\n\nThe line $4y+x=-8$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253429 1072 #uuid "195e8e21-ea85-5ab6-a113-abb18c6e98d1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253429 1074 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)+x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-0 & =-8 \\\\ 4y & =-8 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(-8,0)$ and $(0,-2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{-8+0}{2},\\frac{0-2}{2})=(-4,-1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(-4,-1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(-8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(-4+8)}^{2}+(-1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(-4,-1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{17},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-(-4))}^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{17})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253430 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252906 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253430 1069 "q-117859" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253430 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253430 1071 "![](images/c1/c100b27df3e4d9b23846961ad5d168fbe6f0eecd94ab8a9b99f51e82bca064a3.png)\n\nThe line $2y-3x=-24$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of a circle, find the equation of the circle." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253430 1072 #uuid "7c63574e-df7e-5af6-a537-b5b20d65f6d2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253430 1074 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-3x & =-24 \\\\ -3x & =-24 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3(0) & =-24 \\\\ 2y & =-24 \\\\ y & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(8,0)$ and $(0,-12)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{8+0}{2},\\frac{0-12}{2})=(4,-6).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(4,-6)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(8,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(4-8)}^{2}+(-6-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{52} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(4,-6)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{13},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+(y-(-6))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{13})}^{2} \\\\ (x-4)^{2}+(y+6)^{2} & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252838 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253431 1069 "q-117888" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253431 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253431 1071 "![](images/58/5819e0605dacfb190c2767c4f50a671eb592832aa6a3a1970a8bcdff2790fd19.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253431 1072 #uuid "3de18967-1c79-524e-b070-80260fcd64fd" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253431 1074 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/ad/ad5bf0001a37cf5bebf3288ffbf1fa6bb69747f28f8a81d08d0ffab8223e1707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{21}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{21}$ units to the right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{21}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253432 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252958 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253432 1069 "q-117890" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253432 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253432 1071 "![](images/d5/d54c590d8d64670c0fb90baea864d154cb4c59cd69d7f57234bfd8a5c4a3bfe7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253432 1072 #uuid "3e0561fe-63de-5941-8751-b8579e1f5296" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253432 1074 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+4C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/33/336c38a44a3a40fcaf4a226f61a9f7af2cc731674c1ccc6679b80d846d4ea657.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})$ by $0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n4C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253433 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253131 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253433 1069 "q-117891" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253433 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253433 1071 "![](images/43/4374d7ede23ffbb78ed861d8ebdf116126c546490a87771505cfbe121b973247.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253433 1072 #uuid "68a48b4f-4833-5c9d-96bb-4f430bff0952" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253433 1074 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(x+\\frac{C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/ca/cac3ea6b8409290b6716a194325a885672e06572c1f8a8b63b48b6807f514d42.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253015 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253434 1069 "q-11790" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253434 1071 "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(6,4).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253434 1072 #uuid "cd21d19c-e94c-54e2-a9f4-0d018bf794c2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253434 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,4),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 36 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252934 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253435 1069 "q-11791" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253435 1071 "![](images/48/48ae8debc8c92587e19b6c1cb67e426ef2fcf8a91d75a694a117c5e649cbd3f8.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253435 1072 #uuid "27084a71-9ed1-579f-859f-319625b653b4" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253435 1074 "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-5).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-5,-5),$ so we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-5$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-5)^{2} & =4p(-5) \\\\ 25 & =-20p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{5}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252948 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253436 1069 "q-11792" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253436 1071 "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-5,5).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253436 1072 #uuid "e0b80741-e06b-5c43-b887-c4210becc6d2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253436 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-5,5),$ so we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=5$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-5)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 25 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252881 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253437 1069 "q-11794" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253437 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,4)$ and passes through the point $(-1,8).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253437 1072 #uuid "f5a78323-0f8e-5b1d-8bfd-600ef8cd7e86" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253437 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,4),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,8),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=8$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (8-4)^{2} & =4p(-1-1) \\\\ 16 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4(-2)(x-1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-8(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252858 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253438 1069 "q-117941" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253438 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y-2x,\\,y(2)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253438 1072 #uuid "2d94e599-9559-5a1d-8d84-d50625519544" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253438 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=2$ to $x=4$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y-2x=3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =2+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y-2x=3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+6=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $10$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈10.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252836 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253439 1069 "q-117942" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253439 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253439 1072 #uuid "af0427a6-d3d0-5efe-950b-968ece9492c2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253439 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2}=\\frac{{2}^{2}\\cdot 0}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2}=\\frac{{2}^{2}\\cdot 1}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈4.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253251 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253440 1069 "q-117949" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253440 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253440 1072 #uuid "3347ff99-4404-5530-8977-38f3af2a55ee" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253440 1074 "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |