e values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+0=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253444 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253031 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253444 1069 "q-117982" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253444 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253444 1071 "![](images/fe/fe5c82553a127e5b7690d13114e7f06cae20eaea83e0a4d62b994bb4c64a27d6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253444 1072 #uuid "2f8cbd18-2ea5-5b52-845f-492e8288e7a1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253444 1074 "The diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n![](images/e4/e463bb775deea7ce2f2840d6c3554fffbb93483c6c8694933e78c3c89cca9711.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(x+1)^{2}(1-y) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(x+1)(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $y=1$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<1$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<-1$ | $x>-1$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+1)^{2}(1-y).\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253445 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253094 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253445 1069 "q-117985" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253445 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253445 1071 "![](images/03/03a826b548db2731a6854327d6e11dcc5147d5c21db93f0b17b6b131e5163efc.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253445 1072 #uuid "82d0e533-71c5-5fce-ba0e-f6b94cbcf3f4" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253445 1074 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1],$ the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [-1,3],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-1,3].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius of $2,$ so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (3,5].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =8-2\\pi -2 \\\\  & =6-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252855 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253446 1069 "q-11799" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253446 1071 "![](images/87/872c4385f2f7075ac4f9690f5adabaa8cb6b4ad3ffe738b47f2b22e477aab621.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253446 1072 #uuid "36fb798c-df79-55a4-8a2e-364abb45d1ef" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253446 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,-4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(4,-4),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=-4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (-6+4)^{2} & =4p(0-4) \\\\ 4 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+4)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})(x-4) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253447 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252909 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253447 1069 "q-11800" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253447 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253447 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-4,-1)$ and passes through the point $(5,2).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253447 1072 #uuid "276d74cc-81ab-53f8-848e-d3cdf1f3ff74" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253447 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,-1),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(5,2),$ so we can substitute $x=5$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (2+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =36p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{4})(x+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252864 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253448 1069 "q-11801" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253448 1071 "![](images/d4/d4d0ad25fe8409b55ad4021c5e06d0f13579cea804b1a2b78b18b3ba76bd0c0e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253448 1072 #uuid "9d9f39bd-2803-56ae-905c-30bd79159a52" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253448 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-4,2)$ and passes through the point $(2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,6),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (6-2)^{2} & =4p(2+4) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})(x+4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{2}{3}$ is positive." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253449 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253192 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253449 1069 "q-11802" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253449 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253449 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(5,1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253449 1072 #uuid "b010d0fe-74c1-571a-98b5-1a828c1293c0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253449 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(5,1),$ we substitute $h=5$ and $k=1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,5),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=5$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x-5) \\\\ (5-1)^{2} & =4p(-1-5) \\\\ 16 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x-5) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{2}{3})(x-5) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-\\frac{8}{3}(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253450 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253154 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253450 1069 "q-118025" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253450 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253450 1071 "![](images/50/5077a94fe8b5d5435b5bf50d0ea687f5bc87ae95011e22800ccfe473f8af78dd.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=xy-x$\n- ${y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}+2xy+x$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253450 1072 #uuid "f47e52f5-b113-5140-ac0d-d3912c7ac7a7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253450 1074 "![](images/2f/2f7075161363dee2817aeb8aff9e85781a10bd30c926bfff73724992eee699ac.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=-1$ divide the plane into $4$ quarters. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ quarters are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(y+1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-x=x(y-1)\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=1.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}+2xy+x=x(y+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nevaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=-1,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-1$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253451 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252834 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253451 1069 "q-11803" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253451 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253451 1071 "![](images/81/814515b0294849e2689214faa1ac97a4f0bb5134f3209a9fb0b2fdbda5c2a484.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253451 1072 #uuid "f478aeca-2c7f-5ae7-9dbf-a602b464edd0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253451 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(2,2)$ and passes through the point $(-4,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,2),$ we substitute $h=2$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,8),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=8$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (8-2)^{2} & =4p(-4-2) \\\\ 36 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{2})(x-2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-6(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253452 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253084 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253452 1069 "q-11805" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253452 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253452 1071 "![](images/a1/a16f3f2ba78b5aef180c4331d1dd99f37340d2c6d842c1596e35445f1dbf34c5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253452 1072 #uuid "157ae011-ca8e-518d-b6fc-d2c2017281bc" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253452 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-4,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,0),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(8,6),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(8+4) \\\\ 36 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253453 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252824 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253453 1069 "q-11806" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253453 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253453 1071 "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253453 1072 #uuid "aa15fb81-7036-5f0a-83c0-c74afd28471c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253453 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(4,-3),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,0),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 9 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{4})(x-4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =-3(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253454 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252993 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253454 1069 "q-11807" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253454 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253454 1071 "![](images/e4/e4b681e9bdf5fa8021ca359e0bf93c27b53a18f0d5e906765a911a72ecfc0159.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253454 1072 #uuid "bfe2de45-9f13-54bf-92ae-4e435ecb2b1a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253454 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(-8,10).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(4,2),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,10),$ so we can substitute $x=-8$ and $y=10$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (10-2)^{2} & =4p(-8-4) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{4}{3}$ is negative." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252916 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253455 1069 "q-118075" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253455 1071 "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/3}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253455 1072 #uuid "e1bb6042-8ea9-56a9-af39-690a38b89fcf" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253455 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/3} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253080 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253456 1069 "q-11808" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253456 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-3,1)$ and passes through the point $(0,5).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253456 1072 #uuid "c384e88f-5df3-50d8-843d-d7ee59f7b038" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253456 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-3,1),$ we substitute $h=-3$ and $k=1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,5),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=5$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+3) \\\\ (5-1)^{2} & =4p(0+3) \\\\ 16 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =4p(x+3) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{4}{3})(x+3) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{3}(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252937 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253457 1069 "q-118089" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253457 1071 "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{20nx}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253457 1072 #uuid "c075cefc-4688-5882-8677-50d9b8a4d1b6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253457 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{20nx} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{20x} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{20x} \\\\  & ={e}^{20x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253200 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253458 1069 "q-11809" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253458 1071 "![](images/8a/8a48fcacc437d1dca41df82e24984507a92d686f80f4517c6682e8b14b694f0e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253458 1072 #uuid "582e0262-2ff0-53cf-8f18-89d0545d5f0a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253458 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-6,0)$ and passes through the point $(-2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-6,0),$ we substitute $h=-6$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-2,6),$ so we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+6) \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(-2+6) \\\\ 36 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+6) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})(x+6) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{9}{4}$ is positive." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253010 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253459 1069 "q-118090" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253459 1071 "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nxy}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253459 1072 #uuid "24044cb0-a390-54f5-a384-3ba8bf4f52b0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253459 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\ne={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nxy} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{xy} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252920 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253460 1069 "q-11811" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253460 1071 "![](images/ec/ecf128d341a8a568ade4abaa2a59e1083d2c436fdb3d2a0b4b642fb7e85cee63.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253460 1072 #uuid "fa256900-e0ea-52f1-985a-4c3b8bdded9a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253460 1074 "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(6,4)$ and passes through the point $(1,9).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(6,4),$ we substitute $h=6$ and $k=4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,9),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=9$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6) \\\\ (9-4)^{2} & =4p(1-6) \\\\ 25 & =-20p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{5}{4})(x-6) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-5(x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{5}{4}$ is negative." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252880 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253461 1069 "q-118110" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253461 1071 "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{\\pi n}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253461 1072 #uuid "1b144674-911f-5981-baf0-b97375ed0a69" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253461 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{z}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{\\pi n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{z}{n})}^{n}]}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={({e}^{z})}^{\\pi } \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi z}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252939 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253462 1069 "q-118114" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253462 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{{d}^{2}-3d}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253462 1072 #uuid "8477c0d3-7e97-5832-b4f8-9e356450f509" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253462 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(d,\\,d(d-3),\\,d-3)=d(d-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $d=0$ and $d=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)} & =\\frac{2}{d-3} \\\\ d(d-3)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)}) & =d(d-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{d-3} \\\\ \\frac{4d(d-3)}{d}+\\frac{6d(d-3)}{d(d-3)} & =\\frac{2d(d-3)}{d-3} \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{d}\\cdot (d-3)}{\\cancel{d}}+\\frac{6\\cdot \\cancel{d(d-3)}}{\\cancel{d(d-3)}} & =\\frac{2d\\cdot \\cancel{(d-3)}}{\\cancel{d-3}} \\\\ 4(d-3)+6 & =2d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $d,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(d-3)+6 & =2d \\\\ 4d-6 & =2d \\\\ 2d & =6 \\\\ d & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $d=3$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253003 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253463 1069 "q-118117" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253463 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{{v}^{2}+4v}=\\frac{3}{v}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253463 1072 #uuid "686ef7d4-296f-572a-a3ca-d6455dccd76c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253463 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}=\\frac{3}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(v+4,\\,v(v+4),\\,v)=v(v+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $v=0$ and $v=-4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3}{v} \\\\ v(v+4)\\cdot (\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}) & =v(v+4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v(v+4)}{v+4}+\\frac{12v(v+4)}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3v(v+4)}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v\\cdot \\cancel{(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v+4}}+\\frac{12\\cdot \\cancel{v(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v(v+4)}} & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{v}\\cdot (v+4)}{\\cancel{v}} \\\\ 6v+12 & =3(v+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $v,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6v+12 & =3(v+4) \\\\ 6v+12 & =3v+12 \\\\ 3v & =0 \\\\ v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $v=0$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252986 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253464 1069 "q-11812" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253464 1071 "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(1,2).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253464 1072 #uuid "7d7e628d-39ea-5461-9b3a-d34dcc6784ed" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253464 1074 "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,-3),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,2),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (2+3)^{2} & =4p(1+4) \\\\ 25 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =5(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253089 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253465 1069 "q-118123" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253465 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{{z}^{2}-z}=\\frac{1}{2z}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253465 1072 #uuid "d3e07973-1de0-5078-b548-53734ee05751" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253465 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}=\\frac{1}{2z}.\n$$\n\nWe make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(z-1,\\,2z,\\,z(z-1))=2z(z-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $z=0$ and $z=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ 2z(z-1)\\cdot (\\frac{5}{z-1}-\\frac{5}{z(z-1)}) & =2z(z-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z(z-1)}{z-1}-\\frac{10z(z-1)}{z(z-1)} & =\\frac{2z(z-1)}{2z} \\\\ \\frac{10z\\cdot \\cancel{(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z-1}}-\\frac{10\\cdot \\cancel{z(z-1)}}{\\cancel{z(z-1)}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{2z}\\cdot (z-1)}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ 10z-10 & =z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $z,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10z-10 & =z-1 \\\\ 9z & =9 \\\\ z & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $z=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252907 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253466 1069 "q-118128" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253466 1071 "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-6n}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253466 1072 #uuid "44d44261-a717-51f0-9510-f6aa6afd3eb6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253466 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-6n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-6} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{-6} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{-6} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{6x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252904 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253467 1069 "q-118135" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253467 1071 "${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n{y}^{2}}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253467 1072 #uuid "620596ff-d0c1-5ad6-bffe-e964c03d3a0b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253467 1074 "Recall the following special limit: \n\n$$\n{e}^{x}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{n}]}^{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={({e}^{x})}^{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{x{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252926 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253468 1069 "q-118166" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253468 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)}=\\frac{1}{b-3}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253468 1072 #uuid "5be88614-2f37-5e45-8420-0b1cfbd655e9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253468 1074 "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(b,\\,b(b-3),\\,b-3)=b(b-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $b=0$ and $b=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)} & =\\frac{1}{b-3} \\\\ b(b-3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{b}-\\frac{7}{b(b-3)}) & =b(b-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{b-3} \\\\ \\frac{b(b-3)}{b}-\\frac{7b(b-3)}{b(b-3)} & =\\frac{b(b-3)}{b-3} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{b}\\cdot (b-3)}{\\cancel{b}}-\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{b(b-3)}}{\\cancel{b(b-3)}} & =\\frac{b\\cdot \\cancel{(b-3)}}{\\cancel{b-3}} \\\\ (b-3)-7 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $b,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(b-3)-7 & =b \\\\ b-10 & =b \\\\ -10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above resulted in a false statement, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253038 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253469 1069 "q-118173" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253469 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{{r}^{2}-r}-\\frac{1}{r-1}=\\frac{1}{3r}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253469 1072 #uuid "095a0a7e-34d8-54e9-b5ce-745cce9de220" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253469 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{1}{r-1}=\\frac{1}{3r}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(r(r-1),\\,r-1,\\,3r)=3r(r-1).\n$$\n\n- The values $r=0$ and $r=1$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{1}{r-1} & =\\frac{1}{3r} \\\\ 3r(r-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{1}{r-1}) & =3r(r-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3r} \\\\ \\frac{3r(r-1)}{r(r-1)}-\\frac{3r(r-1)}{r-1} & =\\frac{3r(r-1)}{3r} \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{r(r-1)}}{\\cancel{r(r-1)}}-\\frac{3r\\cdot \\cancel{(r-1)}}{\\cancel{r-1}} & =\\frac{\\cancel{3r}\\cdot (r-1)}{\\cancel{3r}} \\\\ 3-3r & =r-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-3r & =r-1 \\\\ 4 & =4r \\\\ r & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $r=1$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252973 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253470 1069 "q-118179" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253470 1071 "Solve the equation $\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2{h}^{2}+6h}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253470 1072 #uuid "aeddba54-5344-5f11-999d-95a273629db0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253470 1074 "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)}=\\frac{1}{h+3}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(h,\\,2h(h+3),\\,h+3)=2h(h+3).\n$$\n\n- The values $h=0$ and $h=-3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)} & =\\frac{1}{h+3} \\\\ 2h(h+3)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{h}-\\frac{1}{2h(h+3)}) & =2h(h+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{h+3} \\\\ \\frac{2h(h+3)}{h}-\\frac{2h(h+3)}{2h(h+3)} & =\\frac{2h(h+3)}{h+3} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{h}\\cdot (h+3)}{\\cancel{h}}-\\frac{\\cancel{2h(h+3)}}{\\cancel{2h(h+3)}} & =\\frac{2h\\cdot \\cancel{(h+3)}}{\\cancel{h+3}} \\\\ 2(h+3)-1 & =2h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $h,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(h+3)-1 & =2h \\\\ 2h+6-1 & =2h \\\\ 5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above resulted in a false statement, our equation has no solutions." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253066 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253471 1069 "q-11819" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253471 1071 "![](images/71/7129baa9dcd41738dbdf04a022b8bbe6a864006896cc76cbee3594ff0a744608.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253471 1072 #uuid "225fdeec-6ec4-5722-b513-a61764260ea5" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253471 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(0,-2).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+0)^{2} & =4p(y-(-2)) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4p(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(8,6),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y+2) \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(6+2) \\\\ 64 & =32p \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y+2) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(2)(y+2) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =8(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253472 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253014 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253472 1069 "q-11822" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253472 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253472 1071 "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-2).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253472 1072 #uuid "ca3b7f51-a5a6-56e7-8463-29fa6d537419" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253472 1074 "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-1,-4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-1))^{2} & =4p(y-(-4)) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+4) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253473 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252879 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253473 1069 "q-11824" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253473 1071 "Given that $y=(x+1){e}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253473 1072 #uuid "ab66beff-1db7-5265-a536-ea4d30aeb123" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253473 1074 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+1$ and $v(x)={e}^{9x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=9{e}^{9x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(1)({e}^{9x})+(x+1)(9{e}^{9x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{9x}+9(x+1){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(1+9(x+1)){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(9x+10){e}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252856 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253474 1069 "q-11825" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253474 1071 "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,4)$ and which passes through the point $(4,-8).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253474 1072 #uuid "93e2b546-25a2-5a6e-b5f8-8c32e0bcc85a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253474 1074 "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(0,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,-8),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-8-4) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y-4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253475 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253087 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253475 1069 "q-11827" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253475 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253475 1071 "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(3,-5)$ and which passes through the point $(0,4).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253475 1072 #uuid "a291d23e-d654-581b-aa5a-090e3a807669" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253475 1074 "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(3,-5).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-(-5)) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,4),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (0-3)^{2} & =4p(4+5) \\\\ 9 & =36p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{4})(y+5) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =y+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253476 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253134 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253476 1069 "q-11828" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253476 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253476 1071 "![](images/4b/4b30029c03e020e6deacdf6d0728978c5a6d86d88e6bbbf1f8c1a6ec88c278d6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253476 1072 #uuid "49fe4762-714b-5c6d-b7d9-225a3c1ddebb" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253476 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,-2).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+2).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,4),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4p(y+2) \\\\ (0-4)^{2} & =4p(4+2) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4p(y+2) \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})(y+2) \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253477 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253058 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253477 1069 "q-11833" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253477 1071 "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,1)$ and which passes through the point $(-6,-6).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253477 1072 #uuid "6128a71e-f744-5ae8-9df3-81f671cec4ad" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253477 1074 "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(1,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-6,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (-6-1)^{2} & =4p(-6-1) \\\\ 49 & =-28p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{7}{4})(y-1) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-7(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253190 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253478 1069 "q-118348" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253478 1071 "![](images/f2/f2d3945780d6d042ae5fa01905cf7740fca0f5f80307124556a1b4724f691683.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253478 1072 #uuid "8212d812-45a7-565f-b087-e2f78df1febe" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253478 1074 "![](images/6b/6ba976f250811d1ba3109f4aecbfdb2a4de4ee86300fa867e80e2087aa21601f.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{7}{4}$ to give $y=-\\frac{7}{4}sin⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\frac{7}{4}sin⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253479 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252889 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253479 1069 "q-11835" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253479 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253479 1071 "![](images/28/2819ceb4214f8df19ef349794c243771a1080bccf0bf2bbdbdbca9be67d4fbdb.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253479 1072 #uuid "f807a98f-968e-51ff-a643-2a1ff4add6c7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253479 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-4,6).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}=4p(y-6).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (4+4)^{2} & =4p(-6-6) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253480 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252956 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253480 1069 "q-118350" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253480 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253480 1071 "![](images/a0/a065146d50b16eade39c758b59e13994272cf7180aaa662008a1865e4160421e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253480 1072 #uuid "c5a416a1-a537-5d81-9140-46bc32e0a299" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253480 1074 "![](images/46/46e8f92e758c7f4643b64c05d0b457f7b99b6723ddf9be9cd63da28079b89618.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, compress it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ to give $y=-\\frac{1}{4}sin⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{4}sin⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253224 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253481 1069 "q-118352" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253481 1071 "![](images/92/92f4cd47e9046d9fcd37ac105d89c5a78c0c3635ab66c3a1a194e7d24e1f3f39.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253481 1072 #uuid "c17ec7a1-cf6a-525c-a5a6-62aa532c5d59" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253481 1074 "![](images/8c/8c4b58c0e0508fbea7df9619b8086fd70d1a367df36e31d0d7b9ecc7eb0f4378.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-cos⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, compress it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $0.5$ to give $y=-0.5cos⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-0.5cos⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252936 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253482 1069 "q-11837" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253482 1071 "![](images/e7/e72088788a2805da38d53f6c4d4080c6d91e10658d0d54c4b5953ce259e9637c.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253482 1072 #uuid "70aaae45-b7b1-5777-aad2-00ca83c89b47" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253482 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-6,-6).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}=4p(y+6).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2} & =4p(y+6) \\\\ (0+6)^{2} & =4p(-2+6) \\\\ 36 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+6)^{2} & =4p(y+6) \\\\ (x+6)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})(y+6) \\\\ (x+6)^{2} & =9(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252839 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253483 1069 "q-118387" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253483 1071 "![](images/3a/3ae609102d5545d3db2e035238f030e3ee1b58be8aba716224d5df967ca6ed5a.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253483 1072 #uuid "aaabba1b-1cf6-581c-b6c1-fff29f9ff6a9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253483 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)}{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252811 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253484 1069 "q-11839" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253484 1071 "![](images/3d/3dd6c2de764893839a2d3f817ab08faae32e1b2a09e031d4dfb897782917b8af.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253484 1072 #uuid "04ab9a5b-6837-52c0-be78-03f3cf72c5af" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253484 1074 "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(9,-5),$ so we can substitute $x=9$ and $y=-5$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (9-4)^{2} & =4p(-5) \\\\ 25 & =-20p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{5}{4})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253027 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253485 1069 "q-11841" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253485 1071 "Given that $f(x)=x\\cdot {3}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253485 1072 #uuid "11c94822-e3fa-5674-8f0a-3b72546b7b89" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253485 1074 "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={3}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}\\cdot {3}^{x}+x\\cdot ({3}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & ={3}^{x}+(x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x}) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x} \\\\  & =(1+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3){3}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252813 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253486 1069 "q-118450" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253486 1071 "![](images/98/98e3dc2a664f5096d2b7c9ba7bf7fc56faa189cd573a3de462e0b249fbc90af6.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253486 1072 #uuid "e1484f94-afd5-5ba7-9351-7b1e33dca84c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253486 1074 "![](images/84/8499856915baa0bf144561dd35143e024586f4d417ac4f9c1ee48d7586e0f26c.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit **down** to give $y=-sin⁡x-1.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-sin⁡x-1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253133 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253487 1069 "q-118454" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253487 1071 "![](images/d2/d2bda3c0e8f9d19c67074209a9f42617b5814dae505e7f917a5a6f89012b8a87.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253487 1072 #uuid "bde136cf-b853-5290-a074-d60c0c0656c3" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253487 1074 "![](images/ac/ac75968867bedffb5ff090a0ca0ab3457e7107fb4b30be36899b79c3e16809b7.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-cos⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit **down** to give $y=-cos⁡x-1.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-cos⁡x-1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253017 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253488 1069 "q-11854" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253488 1071 "![](images/af/af896b84fd035bc81e97ab6214858ec7627c12b7b9ff25f18483839f4e389ba7.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253488 1072 #uuid "054b33c1-48d0-5971-9cd6-626efbf760f9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253488 1074 "The vertex of a left-opening parabola is the right-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/54/5410d736f7bae2f5c968d52083745b91279913045405ad75d154cf6b05bba4bd.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(2,0).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253173 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253489 1069 "q-11855" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253489 1071 "![](images/90/90a9dd37fd2374298859f18770bd0dca334bf3e995786c50110a00ee23c84b1d.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253489 1072 #uuid "08cbdae0-0bbc-5bc8-9074-3ad7ea76e21c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253489 1074 "The vertex of a right-opening parabola is the left-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/0c/0c7c32df76ee3f371f97a3b4a4eed0ce965ded2133ba07b85284143948e76d0c.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(-4,-2).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252991 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253490 1069 "q-118552" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253490 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of Weddell seals in a certain region of Antarctica follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.2y-0.002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible seal population?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253490 1072 #uuid "374e2fb9-5e2c-5d76-b0e3-c554c19e0eff" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253490 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252886 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253491 1069 "q-118556" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253491 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of chimpanzees in a Brazilian forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(1-\\frac{y}{500}),\\,y(0)=70,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible chimpanzee population?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253491 1072 #uuid "b74d9046-36ae-5245-9ae7-2e1b28efe2e1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253491 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(1-\\frac{y}{500}) \\\\  & =y(\\frac{500-y}{500}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{500}(500-y) \\\\  & =0.002y(500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $500,$ and we conclude that the number of chimpanzees cannot exceed $500.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253150 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253492 1069 "q-118560" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253492 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of mockingbirds in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.6y-0.0003{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=250,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in months, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of mockingbirds?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253492 1072 #uuid "c0106ce7-741f-50bb-856a-97d86a75d481" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253492 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.6y-0.0003{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0003y(2\\,000-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2\\,000,$ and we conclude that the number of mockingbirds cannot exceed $2\\,000.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252828 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253493 1069 "q-118561" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253493 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of sloths in a forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}),\\,y(0)=135,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible sloth population?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253493 1072 #uuid "5e169e8f-e056-58a3-8af0-53918e153ecf" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253493 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =0.3y(2-\\frac{y}{300}) \\\\  & =0.3y(\\frac{600-y}{300}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0.3y}{300}(600-y) \\\\  & =0.001y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of sloths cannot exceed $600.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252903 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253494 1069 "q-118577" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253494 1071 "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,200y-3{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=50\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253494 1072 #uuid "3ee4be40-f62d-562b-9ac0-9147c5c14674" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253494 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y(400-y),\\,y(0)=50.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=400.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $400.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253078 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253495 1069 "q-118580" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253495 1071 "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,750y-7{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=20\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253495 1072 #uuid "9dafb03f-1aec-5b28-9dcd-3ae5e87f428b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253495 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=7y(250-y),\\,y(0)=20.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=250.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $250.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252957 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253496 1069 "q-118581" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253496 1071 "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=720y-8{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=5\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253496 1072 #uuid "edf79f32-6c49-50fc-bc9d-59df10630ea5" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253496 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8y(90-y),\\,y(0)=5.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=90.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $90.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253497 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252969 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253497 1069 "q-118593" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253497 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253497 1071 "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of rhodium-101 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=7{e}^{-0.21t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of rhodium-101?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253497 1072 #uuid "9c8176e2-daa1-5119-be1c-790a766537c3" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253497 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.21.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.21} \\\\  & ≈3.3\\,\\text{years}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253498 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253241 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253498 1069 "q-118595" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253498 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253498 1071 "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of curium-240 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=8{e}^{-0.025\\,67t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of curium-240?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253498 1072 #uuid "53af0d64-6e1c-5e41-839f-478464bfc53e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253498 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.025\\,67.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.025\\,67} \\\\  & ≈27.0\\,\\text{days}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252840 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253499 1069 "q-118602" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253499 1071 "The half-life of francium-224 is $3.33\\,\\text{min}.$ Rounded to the nearest minute, how long does it take for a $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of francium-224 to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253499 1072 #uuid "2b956690-de59-5290-885d-ea6f0bba2eb6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253499 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=3.33\\,\\text{min},$ we have\n\n$$\n3.33=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=160\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n20=160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20 & =160{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{20}{160} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/3.33}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3.33} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3.33\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{20\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{160\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $160\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 3.33\\,\\text{min}≈10\\,\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $10\\,\\text{min}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $20\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253157 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253500 1069 "q-118618" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253500 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253500 1072 #uuid "bfa67a4d-773b-5a56-87ac-2e520036ce73" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253500 1074 "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253221 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253501 1069 "q-118678" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253501 1071 "![](images/3d/3d24ad621be3a812ddf2ed7098ab05598e3ef21ab759519197044511f27b8bbd.png)\n\nGiven $△PQR$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{RQ}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253501 1072 #uuid "e1230231-b61d-5641-a91e-b8d7f9cd4827" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253501 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{RQ}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $P$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{RQ},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{RQ}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/7a/7a3ff69fe62cd06ad829104c3d02f75dd9665c049b9a7a9d17206e9439023088.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252924 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253502 1069 "q-118685" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253502 1071 "![](images/3e/3e83667f215b45dbffc31433c1a622782eaf0b33f85a26a1a08f38d6ac5f0c7c.png)\n\nGiven $△PQR$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{RQ}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253502 1072 #uuid "d5ac4391-f131-5c8c-ab6e-9bd3bfa6452f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253502 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{RQ}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $P$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{RQ},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{RQ}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/3f/3fc454fd77a50a2e1b97f04c8888e4887904493265816a77df537b88e4c4b9e1.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253203 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253503 1069 "q-118711" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253503 1071 "![](images/8f/8faa4d9c078379aa0a74ab25ff235c68cce809c532a0ad38bfb14c045b4fd877.png)\n\nGiven $△JKL$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{KL}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253503 1072 #uuid "cb784875-1e4e-5fb7-a306-93faadab9fdf" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253503 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{KL}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $J$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{KL},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{KL}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{KL}.$\n\n![](images/8f/8fa817d73f39106248403b6690757a701b2bdaf6e1b189a46572201d36f64554.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $J$ to $\\overset{⟷}{KL}.$\n\n![](images/73/737e030ba35f922fc67ed421bec7c072e5d2b6b2d1fda11d2f081453b541d4a8.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253069 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253504 1069 "q-118723" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253504 1071 "At a geological site, a sample of a lunar rock is found with $99%$ of its original rubidium-87 content. Given that the half-life of rubidium-87 is $49.72$ billion years, approximately how old is the sample?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253504 1072 #uuid "b748b211-122b-52ae-a689-406a274e5db9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253504 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=49.72$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n49.72=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.99{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.99{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.99{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.99\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.99 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{49.72\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈0.721\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $0.721$ billion years old." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253505 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252944 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253505 1069 "q-118727" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253505 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253505 1071 "At a geological site, an object is found with $95%$ of its original lutetium-176 content. Given that the half-life of lutetium-176 is $37.64$ billion years, approximately how old is the object?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253505 1072 #uuid "053a3dd7-e1ee-5447-95a8-6095d5866de7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253505 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=37.64$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n37.64=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{37.64}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.95{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.95{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.95{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ 0.95\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ 0.95 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/37.64}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{37.64} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{37.64\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.95)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈2.785\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $2.785$ billion years old." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253506 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253178 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253506 1069 "q-118729" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253506 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253506 1071 "At a geological site, an object is found with $75%$ of its original chlorine-36 content. Given that the half-life of chlorine-36 is $301$ thousand years, approximately how old is the object?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253506 1072 #uuid "2663349a-0b4c-5fc3-ad79-932aae958959" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253506 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=301$ thousand years, we have\n\n$$\n301=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{301}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.75{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.75{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.75\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ 0.75 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/301}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{301} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{301\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.75)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the object is approximately $125$ thousand years old." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253507 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253065 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253507 1069 "q-118755" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253507 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253507 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253507 1072 #uuid "260fa3cd-f59d-50c6-a56e-8cfbd29393f0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253507 1074 "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253508 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252928 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253508 1069 "q-118756" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253508 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253508 1071 "![](images/b2/b26ce8599615bfda6d11efa20d0e6a1bb776af3908751be7912c73d5876d6cbc.png)\n\nConsider $△PQR$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{RH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PQ}.$ If $m∠PQR={122}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠QRH.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253508 1072 #uuid "9589fcf0-92e3-5101-8137-2938ff53a093" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253508 1074 "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HQR+m∠PQR & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR+{122}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR & ={58}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△QHR$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRH+m∠HQR+m∠RHQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{58}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{148}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH & ={32}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253257 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253509 1069 "q-118757" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253509 1071 "![](images/95/95b534f8c159b37884e32b730d9aa502565ff4971cd7d08903ffbfaa94b76623.png)\n\nConsider $△PQR$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{RH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PQ}.$ If $m∠PQR={118}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠QRH.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253509 1072 #uuid "574c517e-3266-5464-9df0-9e740c923895" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253509 1074 "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HQR+m∠PQR & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR+{118}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR & ={62}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△QHR$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRH+m∠HQR+m∠RHQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{62}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{152}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH & ={28}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252893 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253510 1069 "q-118761" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253510 1071 "Consider the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253510 1072 #uuid "96df78fc-91b5-5b18-90a4-e309d8513bb9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253510 1074 "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({a}^{2})$ oscillates between $-1$ and $1.$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253243 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253511 1069 "q-118774" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253511 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253511 1072 #uuid "c8456d00-1e90-5830-966e-53837304687a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253511 1074 "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-{e}^{-{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}-(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}})) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252989 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253512 1069 "q-118776" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253512 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253512 1072 #uuid "64a6785a-7bbf-5956-bb67-37d306305466" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253512 1074 "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=3+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2(3+0)}-\\frac{1}{2(3+{a}^{2})}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253135 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253513 1069 "q-118777" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253513 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253513 1072 #uuid "21a2ef07-2c09-57fc-ace5-9d591df7b8d5" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253513 1074 "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253009 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253514 1069 "q-118779" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253514 1071 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={x}^{8}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253514 1072 #uuid "81cebd09-5186-5bea-8883-e39dc896a0be" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253514 1074 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ which has order $6.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $6.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253032 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253515 1069 "q-11878" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253515 1071 "![](images/2b/2b78b376173589b18d5e8da73897d9552f41a00aab523c0e76892623b586eb53.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253515 1072 #uuid "acdf7c4b-4f3a-5142-ba6b-26da51bdc76a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253515 1074 "The vertex of a downward-opening parabola is the highest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/3c/3c547f893dc1cf2e71c17022ea7c81034214d7aedabe78bf719ebd44b96da329.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(4,8).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252808 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253516 1069 "q-118781" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253516 1071 "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{5}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253516 1072 #uuid "345cfcae-2112-5c63-808e-4798d18f1830" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253516 1074 "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253172 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253517 1069 "q-118783" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253517 1071 "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-x$\n- ${y}^{‴}-y=x-{y}^{″}$\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x=y$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253517 1072 #uuid "412d07fd-1588-561a-99e8-080a2cb49d3e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253517 1074 "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253013 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253518 1069 "q-118787" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253518 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-x=-7y,\\,y(2)=3$\n- ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(0)=0$\n- $x{y}^{″}-6y=14,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253518 1072 #uuid "4247b2af-dd08-543e-82b1-e9616731f453" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253518 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-x=-7y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{4}{y}^{‴}+3y={2}^{y}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1,$ and ${y}^{″}(0)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $x{y}^{″}-6y=14$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253183 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253519 1069 "q-118788" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253519 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $6{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253519 1072 #uuid "941e9c2c-1b18-5669-bb2b-964e53cd46ef" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253519 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253092 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253520 1069 "q-118790" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253520 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253520 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-9x,\\,y(1)=-4$\n- ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y,\\,y(0)=-3$\n- ${y}^{″}+y=3$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253520 1072 #uuid "1c7ca5ad-bcbb-5515-be28-446ac5363a18" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253520 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}=-9x$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=-4.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}-8{x}^{3}=-y$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253129 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253521 1069 "q-118791" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253521 1071 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253521 1072 #uuid "7204e641-7358-50b0-b9c2-d492a160bc98" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253521 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253123 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253522 1069 "q-1188" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253522 1071 "Which set is a subset of $B={a,b,c,f}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253522 1072 #uuid "3f87155c-ad21-52b4-b2e4-c530b2e9673b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253522 1074 "A set is a subset of $B$ if all its elements are elements of $B.$\n\nOf the given sets, only ${c,f}$ has all of its elements in $B.$\n\nTherefore, ${c,f}$ is a subset of $B.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252987 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253523 1069 "q-11880" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253523 1071 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}-6x-2y+19=0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253523 1072 #uuid "ebd022ce-c22d-5a95-8742-a4cebd7fb20b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253523 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x-2y+19 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x & =2y-19 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9 & =2y-19 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =2y-19+9 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =2y-10 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =2(y-5) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(3,5).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252983 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253524 1069 "q-11884" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253524 1071 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $3{x}^{2}-30x-8y+67=0.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253524 1072 #uuid "75271eed-aac2-5e60-97ce-6705a0cbc81a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253524 1074 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-30x-8y+67 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x & =8y-67 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}-10x] & =8y-67 \\\\ 3[(x-5)^{2}-25] & =8y-67 \\\\ 3(x-5)^{2}-75 & =8y-67 \\\\ 3(x-5)^{2} & =8y-67+75 \\\\ 3(x-5)^{2} & =8y+8 \\\\ 3(x-5)^{2} & =8(y+1) \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}(y+1) \\\\ (x-5)^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(5,-1).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253169 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253525 1069 "q-118858" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253525 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy}=2$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253525 1072 #uuid "e4fb73e0-2435-50c4-b136-c8ed7ccb224f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253525 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2y}{y}^{′}+1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-{e}^{-2y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-{e}^{-2y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\sqrt{xy} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2-\\sqrt{xy} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2-\\sqrt{xy}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253048 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253526 1069 "q-118859" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253526 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-3{x}^{2}=1$\n- ${y}^{′}+6y={e}^{3y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253526 1072 #uuid "81da2b48-aa38-5eb5-8b7d-567a07263315" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253526 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+6y & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{3y}-6y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={e}^{3y}-6y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252912 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253527 1069 "q-118860" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253527 1071 "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253527 1072 #uuid "f0a2dfb2-a8f3-515c-8e4f-d42e9099dd28" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253527 1074 "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{4}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{4}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\frac{3\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252911 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253528 1069 "q-118868" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253528 1071 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2}=8$\n- ${y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x}=3{y}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253528 1072 #uuid "40dc1226-9a4e-5dff-a14a-c81909c8002d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253528 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+6x{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{8-6x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡({y}^{2}+2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253085 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253529 1069 "q-118869" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253529 1071 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{y}^{2}={x}^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253529 1072 #uuid "f1c949f8-b9ab-5207-8749-bf7052ce14f4" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253529 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{′}}{y}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi } \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=-\\frac{y}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y+2\\pi }.$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253107 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253530 1069 "q-118870" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253530 1071 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{3-y}-2$\n- $y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+y)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253530 1072 #uuid "1e075483-5dc5-5bbb-be2d-e2d1164eea57" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253530 1074 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. It is of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{3-y}-2.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253174 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253531 1069 "q-118885" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253531 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}{cos}^{2}⁡y$ for $0<y<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253531 1072 #uuid "a130453a-6065-5959-8de8-bb802e7d41f1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253531 1074 "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}{cos}^{2}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡y & ={e}^{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡y={e}^{x}+C.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253219 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253532 1069 "q-1189" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253532 1071 "Which of the following sets is a subset of $A={1,2,3,4}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253532 1072 #uuid "85335d23-39b2-5af6-837a-367b385bb13d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253532 1074 "A set is a subset of $A$ if all its elements are elements of $A.$\n\nNotice that the empty set ${}$ is a subset of any set.\n\nTherefore, ${}$ is a subset of $A.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253023 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253533 1069 "q-118944" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253533 1071 "Consider the points $A(-3s,-4s,7s)$ and $B(s,-6s,3s).$ Given that the distance between $A$ and $B$ is $54,$ find the possible values of $s.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253533 1072 #uuid "59b25335-7529-5e36-aa78-a4fb3b713907" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253533 1074 "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(-3s,-4s,7s)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(s,-6s,3s)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(s-(-3s))^{2}+(-6s-(-4s))^{2}+(3s-7s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4s)^{2}+(-2s)^{2}+(-4s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{s}^{2}+4{s}^{2}+16{s}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{s}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\,|s|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=54,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\,|s| & =54 \\\\ |s| & =9 \\\\ s & =\\pm 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252865 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253534 1069 "q-118976" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253534 1071 "![](images/dc/dcd36221e7ad293f0aac1ab99ece80641cb077efd05adc168a552e97d86100c8.png)\n\nIn parallelogram $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AD}=\\overset{\\to }{CB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\overset{\\to }{FA}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253534 1072 #uuid "41da8ad0-e0fc-5f39-8eb7-c4706c551e5a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253534 1074 "- Equality I is false. Indeed, $\\overset{\\to }{AD}\\ne \\overset{\\to }{CB}$ since they have the opposite directions.\n\n- Equality II is true. Indeed, $\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}=\\overset{\\to }{DB}.$\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed, $\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\overset{\\to }{AF}\\ne \\overset{\\to }{FA}$ since they have the opposite directions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252900 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253535 1069 "q-11900" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253535 1071 "Given that $y=5{log}_{4}⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253535 1072 #uuid "b9de855f-8e10-5bff-89a6-c3649ce380b6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253535 1074 "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{xln⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{log}_{4}⁡x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{4}⁡x) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{xln⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252899 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253536 1069 "q-119007" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253536 1071 "What is the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({75}^{∘})?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253536 1072 #uuid "97e8e577-5bc3-50c1-b8c6-66747cc71a22" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253536 1074 "The angle ${75}^{∘}$ is not a special angle. However, we can rewrite it as the sum of two special angles, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{75}^{∘}={45}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $x={45}^{∘}$ and $y={30}^{∘},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{45}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252871 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253537 1069 "q-119032" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253537 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253537 1072 #uuid "b105cb3a-38a2-5c0a-8e78-f3632457e0c5" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253537 1074 "First, notice that the derivative term is already isolated on the left:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253196 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253538 1069 "q-119034" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253538 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{x-1}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253538 1072 #uuid "e46bf143-4783-5ce4-a8e4-31db596b41d7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253538 1074 "First, notice that the derivative term is already isolated on the left:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{3}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253186 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253539 1069 "q-119035" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253539 1071 "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)+6{x}^{2}=4x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253539 1072 #uuid "fb6cc49c-88f2-58a8-a4b7-b8174a349cdf" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253539 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =4x-6{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4x-6{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-6\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}(1-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253165 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253540 1069 "q-119038" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253540 1071 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=8{e}^{-2x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253540 1072 #uuid "31249dfd-dd0f-51ef-9508-c411dcb82eb2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253540 1074 "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =8\\int {e}^{-2x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x})+C \\\\  & =-4{e}^{-2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{-2x}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-4(-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x})+Cx+D \\\\  & =2{e}^{-2x}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252949 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253541 1069 "q-119041" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253541 1071 "Solve the differential equation $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253541 1072 #uuid "39f64cb7-5a70-5fc4-8565-289e61a98a66" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253541 1074 "Integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-8\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-8(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =4\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+Cx+D \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253052 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253542 1069 "q-119042" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253542 1071 "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6x=8{e}^{x/2}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253542 1072 #uuid "4f306795-58e9-56b9-bfb8-50791b341767" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253542 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=8{e}^{x/2}-6x.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (8{e}^{x/2}-6x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-6\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =8(2{e}^{x/2})-6(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\  & =16{e}^{x/2}-3{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (16{e}^{x/2}-3{x}^{2}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =16\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =16(2{e}^{x/2})-3(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})+Cx+D \\\\  & =32{e}^{x/2}-{x}^{3}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253116 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253543 1069 "q-119043" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253543 1071 "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5=6{x}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253543 1072 #uuid "c5323a95-ccb4-5698-8e94-ec2b6a694be3" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253543 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6{x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (6{x}^{2}-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-5x+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-5x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}-5x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253199 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253544 1069 "q-119044" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253544 1071 "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-{2}^{x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253544 1072 #uuid "4f9dee74-c92e-5523-b157-7ba038c745c8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253544 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{2}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{2}^{x})\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{2}^{x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{x}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252976 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253545 1069 "q-119045" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253545 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ that satisfies the condition $f(0)=-1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253545 1072 #uuid "8dd37419-0acf-5e07-951f-998d06a1d1c6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253545 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =-2\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(0)=-1$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+C \\\\ -1 & =-2(0)+C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253161 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253546 1069 "q-119046" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253546 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${f}^{′}(x)-4x=1$ that satisfies the condition $f(1)=-1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253546 1072 #uuid "70e058ec-5742-5754-884d-951409c47fdc" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253546 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4x+1.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (4x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(x) & =4\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+x+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $f(1)=-1$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =2(1)^{2}+1+C \\\\ -1 & =2+1+C \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{2}+x-4.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253119 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253547 1069 "q-119052" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253547 1071 "Tommy drinks $5$ milliliters of vitamin syrup each day from a bottle. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the amount of vitamin syrup $S$ (in milliliters) remaining in the bottle? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in days." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253547 1072 #uuid "43fbe338-3891-5e19-9378-acff0da9bdf4" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253547 1074 "If the amount of vitamin syrup remaining is $S$ milliliters, then the rate of change of the syrup volume is $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in days.\n\nSince Tommy drinks $5$ milliliters of syrup each day, the rate of change of the amount of syrup remaining is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-5.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is negative because the amount of syrup is *decreasing*." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252809 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253548 1069 "q-119053" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253548 1071 "Lucy reads $6$ pages of her new book each day. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the number of pages $P(t)$ Lucy has left to read? Assume that the time $t$ is measured in days." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253548 1072 #uuid "519d33a9-6a9f-594d-baeb-753f6b0e42b2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253548 1074 "If the number of pages Lucy has left to read is $P$, then the rate of change of the number of the pages is $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in days.\n\nSince Lucy reads $6$ pages every day, the rate of change of the number of pages she has left is given by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=-6.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is negative because the number of pages Lucy has left to read is *decreasing*." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253139 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253549 1069 "q-119054" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253549 1071 "The amount of money $M(t)$ in a saving account increases at a rate that is proportional to the amount of money currently in the account. What differential equation describes the rate of change in the amount of money in the saving account $M,$ at time $t?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253549 1072 #uuid "95e72c56-3767-5aa1-b197-f2f305012ad9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253549 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **amount of money** $M(t)$ in a saving account **increases** at a **rate** that is **proportional** to the **amount of money** currently in the saving account.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *amount of money* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *amount of money.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}∝M.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the amount of money *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=kM.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252943 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253550 1069 "q-119075" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253550 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}+{e}^{-2x}=8x$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253550 1072 #uuid "ea7f0932-1ce6-52df-a5dc-f1855b5f815e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253550 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8x-{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (8x-{e}^{-2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =8\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2})+C \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y=4{x}^{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(0)=1$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4(0)^{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-2(0)}}{2}+C \\\\ 1 & =0+\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny=4{x}^{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253122 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253551 1069 "q-119083" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253551 1071 "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}-2x={e}^{x/2}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=2.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253551 1072 #uuid "d5aa7094-d36f-5005-a0c6-3e11c04ef4b9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253551 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+{e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{e}^{x/2})\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{e}^{x/2}+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2{e}^{x/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y={x}^{2}+2{e}^{x/2}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(0)=2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ={0}^{2}+2{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+2(1)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+2{e}^{x/2}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253071 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253552 1069 "q-1191" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253552 1071 "Find all of the subsets of $A={x,y,z}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253552 1072 #uuid "4dc7abe5-6826-51d9-82bd-d471faabcb9f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253552 1074 "To find every subset of $A,$ we list out every combination of elements within $A.$\n\nWe can use the following diagram to visualize the process:\n\n![](images/1d/1d7c11586a8fff5f63d46d936fd32c7b5fbfc33a406ddf268bfdd934fe787cdf.png)\n\nNote that we must include the empty set since it is a subset of every set.\n\nBy doing this, we get\n\n$$\n∅,{x},{y},{z},{x,y},{x,z},{y,z},{x,y,z}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253553 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253228 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253553 1069 "q-11911" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253553 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253553 1071 "Given that $y=2x+ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253553 1072 #uuid "2a7a5557-d240-5298-a285-f0853a897b06" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253553 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+ln⁡x) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253554 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252961 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253554 1069 "q-119132" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253554 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253554 1071 "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253554 1072 #uuid "0b17887e-edc7-5942-b62d-5bad58805bd8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253554 1074 "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-4{e}^{-x/4}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-x/4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=0,3,6.$\n\n![](images/a4/a4b534bd33daabc94aa3c86935b1fd4c0be723fafbc418ed382716541a864293.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253555 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253167 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253555 1069 "q-119181" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253555 1071 "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253555 1072 #uuid "4d8042e9-675f-5529-a27d-041cbf4d9876" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253555 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,-1),(-2,3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-1)}=-2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,3)}=-2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/74/74cf2bd34be1693b5f0ce93303ac0039058a5fda17023dd94836563ea9d0404a.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252882 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253556 1069 "q-119194" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253556 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x+2$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $x=-1,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $x=-2,$ the slope field has a positive slope" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253556 1072 #uuid "19539888-a72b-5a8c-9bb4-399cabb49fb2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253556 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=2(0)+2=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is equal to $2$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,y)}=2(-1)+2=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-1,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(-2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,y)}=2(-2)+2=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is negative for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(-2,y)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d40c405f3f4db66a18e06a5327e86600081a6bc57b502ebed9c94471a0d5857.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253557 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252980 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253557 1069 "q-119199" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253557 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253557 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253557 1072 #uuid "72e807f7-837a-5836-9313-38f63281c554" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253557 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}})]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/11/11c74897fe914599a78c5997767bad83d13ddfb1e35ee66622d8880743688bad.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253558 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253053 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253558 1069 "q-119206" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253558 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}-3x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=2,$  the slope field has a negative slope\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$  the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253558 1072 #uuid "6eca5ac4-aebb-5558-a54f-a273db0d7d0e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253558 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(2,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,y)}={2}^{2}-3(2)=-2,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(2,y)$ is negative for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}={0}^{2}-3(0)=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,1)}={x}^{2}-3x,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,1)$ depends on $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e2ce8d7df3d0ceee4f8c9dcf368940006f1266a751c2f8e5ea8afdcfb90bf7ac.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253559 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253152 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253559 1069 "q-119208" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253559 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253559 1071 "![](images/4f/4f2807a0989a3f36376d1ed93d02162ded72200803d28b49caab701176837628.png)\n\nWhat is the total surface area of the rectangular pyramid represented by the net above?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253559 1072 #uuid "3c0b6ce7-4477-5634-8893-418d10cd145a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253559 1074 "The surface area of our pyramid consists of $1$ square (base) and $4$ triangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =1\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the side}) \\\\  & =1\\times 36+4\\times 30 \\\\  & =36+120 \\\\  & =156\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252823 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253560 1069 "q-119209" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253560 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-{x}^{3}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=1,$  the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$  the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $y=2,$ the slope of the slope field varies" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253560 1072 #uuid "5e9d7c3e-9e55-5477-a8a9-aed5b3e5371d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253560 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,y)}=1-{1}^{3}=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(1,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,y)}=1-{0}^{3}=1>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(0,y)$ is positive for any $y.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is equal to $1$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=1-{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,2)$ depends on $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85d8f71c4680a56d5722b87fa5f7b2a7623fd2457f540c2cd15cd3bfa29694f3.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253561 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253106 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253561 1069 "q-119231" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253561 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253561 1071 "The number of subscribers $S(t)$ to a certain site's e-mail news service after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.01S.\n$$\n\nIf the site's e-mail news service currently has $10\\,000$ subscribers, calculate the number of subscribers after $10$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253561 1072 #uuid "7681f328-523f-50bf-b697-22aa240e7260" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253561 1074 "We model the number of subscribers using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.01S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{0.01t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that the service currently has $10\\,000$ subscribers, so we have ${S}_{0}=10\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of subscribers $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=10\\,000\\,{e}^{0.01t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of subscribers when $t=10,$ we substitute $t=10$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(10) & =10\\,000\\,{e}^{0.01\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =10\\,000\\,{e}^{0.1} \\\\  & ≈11\\,051.71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that the number of subscribers is $11\\,052$ after $10$ weeks." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253562 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252962 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253562 1069 "q-119264" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253562 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253562 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253562 1072 #uuid "19423d48-c141-516d-a17c-c34914d7472c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253562 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253563 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252942 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253563 1069 "q-119269" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253563 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253563 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253563 1072 #uuid "d83726e1-bf9a-535d-b296-5884a6081f79" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253563 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=9y(3-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=9,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=3.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253564 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252829 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253564 1069 "q-119270" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253564 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253564 1071 "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253564 1072 #uuid "7d5335e1-b50d-5f76-8d81-161fb342a580" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253564 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-0.5{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5y(10-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253565 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253151 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253565 1069 "q-119286" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253565 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253565 1071 "The growth rate of the population $P(t)$ in a certain city is jointly proportional to the population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the city's carrying capacity and the population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,8$ and the maximum possible population is $200\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253565 1072 #uuid "7231a27e-cc7a-5b74-bbc6-64de8a2ccd35" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253565 1074 "Since the population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the population, the population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,8,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=200\\,000.$ Therefore, the population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P).\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253566 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252872 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253566 1069 "q-119289" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253566 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253566 1071 "A pest infestation is spreading through a land with $300$ trees. The rate of growth in the number of trees $P(t)$ infected with the pest is jointly proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ measured in months,  and the number of uninfected trees. If the constant of proportionality is $0.2,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253566 1072 #uuid "6e07b3cd-6441-55ba-89c7-eb3d668f886b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253566 1074 "Since the growth rate of infected trees is *jointly* proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected trees, the number of infected trees follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=300.$ Therefore, the number of trees that are infected is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P).\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253567 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253247 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253567 1069 "q-119290" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253567 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253567 1071 "A population of $50\\,000\\,000$ people is being vaccinated against a disease. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ vaccinated is jointly proportional to the number of people who have been vaccinated at time $t,$ measured in months, and the number of unvaccinated people. If the constant of proportionality is $0.3,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253567 1072 #uuid "69d6c7c8-a17d-5595-94e0-1820b1ec12de" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253567 1074 "Since the growth rate of vaccinated people is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have been vaccinated at time $t,$ and the number of unvaccinated people, the number of vaccinated people follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.3,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have been vaccinated is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P).\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253568 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253138 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253568 1069 "q-119294" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253568 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253568 1071 "Which of the following **cannot** be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253568 1072 #uuid "c15de267-47d3-5dc2-912e-a5e4ed985709" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253568 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/eb/ebd12a9b01a0eddc75a8e6a638ad68f55f694f980a6bccab91a8e8542a2c9038.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line vary as we go left and right along the line. \n\n![](images/0a/0aae2e69cf46a7df097a7d34c7e53feb51516e3d7b710e65251fb49c3fc80cb7.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253569 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253049 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253569 1069 "q-11930" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253569 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253569 1071 "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡(8x)$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253569 1072 #uuid "b9e0ca00-8b2a-5d3e-84fd-62f2c13cf2f6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253569 1074 "Here $f(x)=ln⁡(8x)$ is a function of a function. We use the chain rule.First, we express $g(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=ln⁡u,\\,u=8x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8x}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{8}}{\\cancel{8}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253570 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253104 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253570 1069 "q-119301" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253570 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253570 1071 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253570 1072 #uuid "d70edcca-bf87-5796-a6c0-aa00b559b610" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253570 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $x$ for a fixed value of $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/14/1497231f27da97063782fa93592b038676810b2d8cc0ad89bb97522b7348a3d5.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a horizontal line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we move left and right along the line.\n\n![](images/0b/0ba4cdd42093e6341f0ec89d56c332b1de4bf6794aeae1f3fd4f77e343bb5f59.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252877 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253571 1069 "q-119305" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253571 1071 "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253571 1072 #uuid "54db2afb-10ce-59cf-8f9b-eaefe7a751a7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253571 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,1),(1,1)$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=-{1}^{2}=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=-{1}^{2}=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,-1)}=-(-1)^{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](images/bb/bb078d2e632ef5cc29f0e03903a148c8caf0ded552ab9b2604c5b3ecf586d0c4.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252869 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253572 1069 "q-119308" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253572 1071 "The point $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253572 1072 #uuid "4d38949b-9d50-5e0f-bb63-ea8ff36967c0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253572 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3+1)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(4))=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-4+4)=(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2)-1=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+4 \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252940 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253573 1069 "q-11932" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253573 1071 "Given that $g(x)=ln⁡{x}^{2}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253573 1072 #uuid "ad2837f1-ee8d-5b88-b0b4-540909ba188a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253573 1074 "First, we express $g(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=ln⁡u,\\,u={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{x}}{x\\cdot \\cancel{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252925 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253574 1069 "q-119324" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253574 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $y=2,$ the slope field has a positive slope\n- At every point along the line $y=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n- At every point along the line $y=-2,$ the slope field has a negative slope" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253574 1072 #uuid "95486801-04a3-50a0-ad5b-8d31467cc59e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253574 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThis is an autonomous equation because the right-hand side is a function of $y$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $y,$ the slope does not vary with $x.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,2)}=-2<0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is negative for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,2)$ is equal to $-2$ for any $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(x,0)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,0)}=-0=0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,0)$ is horizontal for any $x.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,-2)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,-2)}=-(-2)=2>0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is positive for any $x.$ Moreover, the slope at the point $(x,-2)$ is equal to $2$ for any $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\nWe can use this information to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8f/8fc0b5be5c9147c29410ff7a082316403302f2bce7ec25c665ab7ccc5bf90b0f.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252866 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253575 1069 "q-119341" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253575 1071 "The number of sales $S(t)$ of a new tech device $t$ weeks after the start of sales can be modeled by the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1S.\n$$\n\nIf there were $100\\,000$ sales initially, calculate the number of sales after $10$ weeks. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253575 1072 #uuid "45aa4aa2-7cf9-5de2-abd9-971473e01594" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253575 1074 "We model the number of sales using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=-0.1S.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nS(t)={S}_{0}\\,{e}^{-0.1t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${S}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that there were $100\\,000$  sales initially, so we have ${S}_{0}=100\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the number of sales $S$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nS(t)=100\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.1t}\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of sales when $t=10,$ we substitute $t=10$ into the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(10) & =100\\,000\\,{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =100\\,000\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈36\\,787.94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRounding our answer to the nearest integer, we conclude that there are $36\\,788$ sales after $10$ weeks." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252854 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253576 1069 "q-119361" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253576 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253576 1072 #uuid "6494dcea-ff4c-57dc-b8ab-235d70e87dde" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253576 1074 "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1+({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =2.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253213 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253577 1069 "q-119362" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253577 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression ${cos}^{3}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253577 1072 #uuid "254c5821-755d-5778-be48-e9e74787cc4a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253577 1074 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253002 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253578 1069 "q-119367" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253578 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253578 1072 #uuid "13700841-907d-5c85-a921-c73f7e19a68c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253578 1074 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253100 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253579 1069 "q-119369" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253579 1071 "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-{x}^{2}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253579 1072 #uuid "a04c1b90-953c-573f-89ab-d961bb6587e1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253579 1074 "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/1b/1bd04b981c412d771617ffaaa7cedf88b063ae49ab567cd27d665eb381a1b648.png)\n\n  \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/83/83f2ac528d0e4bdd7e486284a9f3d5692e0111c5cdc58dcc554e12dd0185f15f.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253096 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253580 1069 "q-119371" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253580 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression ${sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253580 1072 #uuid "1e264613-6b71-5c6c-b1c0-6da9257b1f1a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253580 1074 "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, we use ${sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253112 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253581 1069 "q-119379" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253581 1071 "$\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253581 1072 #uuid "f206d2c2-ee8e-56e4-8de0-ea23491335cf" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253581 1074 "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252853 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253582 1069 "q-119381" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253582 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253582 1072 #uuid "7483895f-9f2e-5510-9cf9-357e34d2424a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253582 1074 "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }}{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253047 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253583 1069 "q-119387" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253583 1071 "$\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253583 1072 #uuid "e604df29-4056-5a18-af72-ee9756feedd1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253583 1074 "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253233 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253584 1069 "q-119392" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253584 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253584 1072 #uuid "c41ec619-a5e7-5d9c-af90-cb7a886021e8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253584 1074 "First, we factor $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253026 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253585 1069 "q-11945" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253585 1071 "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253585 1072 #uuid "0145758b-88e9-57c3-9578-3a41888f61e8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253585 1074 "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x+3\\cdot y \\\\ 0\\cdot x+1\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252927 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253586 1069 "q-119451" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253586 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a-b\\sqrt{55}}{42},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253586 1072 #uuid "e1267137-e6ff-5440-b525-7d34538d465f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253586 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{11})^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36-11}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})+(\\frac{5}{6})(\\frac{2}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{55}}{42}+\\frac{10}{42} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-3\\sqrt{55}}{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=10,b=3,$ and $a+b=13.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252894 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253587 1069 "q-119479" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253587 1071 "Find the exact value of  $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{195}^{∘}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${195}^{∘}={135}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}.$*" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253587 1072 #uuid "9b9e46d2-105c-587e-ad59-8da071cc7eea" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253587 1074 "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={135}^{∘}$ and $y={60}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({195}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{135}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253117 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253588 1069 "q-119482" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253588 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253588 1072 #uuid "24fefd74-9f51-579d-ab19-a20613b6de76" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253588 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={30}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253148 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253589 1069 "q-119485" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253589 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(w-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253589 1072 #uuid "8e2c5aed-c37a-55b2-85b1-5c5820fa0a08" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253589 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=w$ and $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(w-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡w\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡w\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡w\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡w-\\operatorname{cos}⁡w).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253043 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253590 1069 "q-11952" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253590 1071 "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253590 1072 #uuid "a4ffeb6b-9180-5a30-8a67-a654a2e3aa3a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253590 1074 "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=2.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $2.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252873 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253591 1069 "q-119597" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253591 1071 "The value $V=V(t)$ of a certain art object $t$ years after its production can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is the growth rate. If it was initially worth $$2\\,000,$ and $V(10)=10\\,000$, calculate the value  of the growth rate $k$ to the nearest percent." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253591 1072 #uuid "74752b29-704c-5e5f-8887-3d2f4bffc5fa" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253591 1074 "We model the value of the art object using the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=kV.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nV(t)={V}_{0}\\,{e}^{kt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${V}_{0}$ is the initial value.\n\nWe're told that  it was initially worth $$2\\,000,$ so we have ${V}_{0}=2\\,000.$\n\nTherefore, the value $V$ at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nV(t)=2\\,000\\,{e}^{kt}.\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $k,$ we substitute $V(10)=10\\,000$ into the equation above and solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(10) & =2\\,000\\,{e}^{k\\cdot 10} \\\\ 10\\,000 & =2\\,000\\,{e}^{10k} \\\\ {e}^{10k} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{2\\,000} \\\\ {e}^{10k} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{10k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ 10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ k & ≈0.16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k≈16%,$ rounded to the nearest percent." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252844 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253592 1069 "q-11961" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253592 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-2,-3),(0,5),(2,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(3,-2)$\n- $(-5,0)$\n- $(5,2)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253592 1072 #uuid "3510664b-2f53-5d5d-9a15-f28784a8e0c6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253592 1074 "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,-2),(-5,0),(5,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252867 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253593 1069 "q-119647" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253593 1071 "At a geological site, a sample of a lunar rock is found with $84%$ of its original rubidium-87 content. Given that the half-life of rubidium-87 is $49.72$ billion years, approximately how old is the sample?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253593 1072 #uuid "16031afa-4de9-5023-a0d4-20bd431ec701" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253593 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=49.72$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n49.72=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=0.84{M}_{0}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n0.84{M}_{0}={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.84{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ 0.84 & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/49.72}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84) & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{49.72} \\\\ t & =-\\frac{49.72\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.84)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & ≈12.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sample is approximately $12.5$ billion years old." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253159 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253594 1069 "q-11967" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253594 1071 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(-1,2),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a **clockwise** rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(0,\\sqrt{2})$\n- $(3,-1)$\n- $(-5,1)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253594 1072 #uuid "34508741-6c7f-54ff-a124-34cef40dd55c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253594 1074 "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are \n\n$$\n(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253056 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253595 1069 "q-119674" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253595 1071 "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253595 1072 #uuid "2e87292a-2b40-5740-b2bc-7f066927b72e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253595 1074 "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,-2),(2,-2)$ and $(2,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{(-1)}=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-2)}=-\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{2}=-2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,2)}=-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/5e/5e6b2df4680cea04cda42312659c76ba713a025150ebcc547dddc2a6501aebf3.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252826 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253596 1069 "q-11969" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253596 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{-x}{9-2x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253596 1072 #uuid "4ae64d20-c5b4-50bb-94bc-ab5da04017e1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253596 1074 "Let $u=-x$ and $v=9-2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =-1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(9-2x)\\cdot (-1)-(-x)\\cdot (-2)}{{(9-2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9+2x-2x}{{(9-2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{(9-2x)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253149 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253597 1069 "q-11972" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253597 1071 "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253597 1072 #uuid "c42e6ce8-2785-5191-9d67-38a51c8bea0b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253597 1074 "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-(-8)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253598 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253101 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253598 1069 "q-11974" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253598 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253598 1071 "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $Q$ is mapped to the quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ has vertices $(-2,-2),(-3,2),(2,2),$ and $(-1,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $Q?$\n\n- $(2,2)$\n- $(0,1)$\n- $(-5,-8)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253598 1072 #uuid "9d13de60-db83-5fa3-ae76-e5805b3d5848" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253598 1074 "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${Q}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $Q$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-(-2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(0,-2),(-5,-8),(0,2),(0,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253599 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252812 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253599 1069 "q-119760" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253599 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253599 1071 "The mass $M(t),$ in micrograms, of a sample of manganese-53 is given by\n\n$$\nM(t)=17{e}^{-0.187t},\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in millions of years. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the half-life of manganese-53?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253599 1072 #uuid "730cc5a0-9aaf-5421-82ce-d1c4dac0713f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253599 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $k=0.187.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1/2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{0.187} \\\\  & ≈3.7\\,\\text{million years}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253037 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253600 1069 "q-119764" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253600 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253600 1072 #uuid "d0d3dca9-aff5-5370-bb54-448ec1d2e38e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253600 1074 "First, we rewrite the first term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253601 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253093 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253601 1069 "q-119766" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253601 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253601 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=6xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253601 1072 #uuid "d92f1990-38b8-544d-a68e-c6daa48b1c4f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253601 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=6xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/6e/6ef852c9c15dc6f591119906abe479949f929edb6dc26f21588b4de545d02da2.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253602 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252897 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253602 1069 "q-119767" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253602 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253602 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253602 1072 #uuid "a82c56b9-720e-52fc-8dc1-2a735f11a49c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253602 1074 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253051 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253603 1069 "q-119769" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253603 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-5xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253603 1072 #uuid "e15411d6-1039-50b8-8bc5-cef852747aab" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253603 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=-5xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253604 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252915 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253604 1069 "q-11977" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253604 1071 "The Unit square $D$ has vertices $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253604 1072 #uuid "2e3715b1-99f4-58e2-8933-aec3faf54b69" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253604 1074 "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](images/68/6805467a24cb40559f205cbfcecc4f5a34ff2ec6ebda8ae67f997c02f123b53c.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=4\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 2=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,2\\,|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253605 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252968 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253605 1069 "q-119770" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253605 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253605 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253605 1072 #uuid "8a4d16bb-7544-56af-8e64-b1dbb3f56d7c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253605 1074 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253606 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252959 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253606 1069 "q-119773" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253606 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253606 1071 "$1+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253606 1072 #uuid "655d2fb5-3fa2-5139-a053-7e43658638e3" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253606 1074 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{1\\cdot (1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1+1}{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{2}{1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253607 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252905 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253607 1069 "q-119774" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253607 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253607 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the first quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253607 1072 #uuid "c47e99b0-49b1-5362-97e1-164913960980" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253607 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/d1/d1e4f48450a7f2032d755a5255d1a1e8a726dd16d273dae5dc1e587826d5ce58.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the first quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the first quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253608 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252982 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253608 1069 "q-119775" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253608 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253608 1071 "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253608 1072 #uuid "a48a9a21-fe38-560f-b14b-f3224e4f215c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253608 1074 "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1}-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=-1.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253609 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253062 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253609 1069 "q-11978" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253609 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253609 1071 "Given that $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253609 1072 #uuid "3000a138-321d-511f-bb2f-c25bd88c5e97" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253609 1074 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=(x+1)^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{2}+1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2(x+1),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(x+1))({x}^{2}+1)-((x+1)^{2})(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)-2x(x+1)^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(({x}^{2}+1)-x(x+1))}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1-{x}^{2}-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(1-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253610 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253083 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253610 1069 "q-119797" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253610 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253610 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(2-x)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>2$ and $y<-1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253610 1072 #uuid "635d0331-704b-5958-9f79-2f8d94fa4111" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253610 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=(2-x)(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=2$ or $y=-1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa61190f1a5565c58838556529715c08f9f00402c6f4b3ccb99a914e748f198.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/01/01e91ecf01cfc64ed87e29081a6b9f8a3fc5afade1b25be08039b8778d981cf7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x>2$ and $y<-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253611 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253211 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253611 1069 "q-119800" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253611 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253611 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(1-x)(y-2)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y>2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<2,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253611 1072 #uuid "dff46479-0340-5239-b8ff-db620f2c9a7a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253611 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=(1-x)(y-2).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=1$ or $y=2.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/47784a3201e93648e495827b12078073cc9f1a03cd3c11904a875bad10a3bde2.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/73/73631c178777b4489e35fa87d08474ccf2b8b484b9bf5fe7272b1ce25d45f1b0.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $x<1$ and $y>2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $x>1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is false. If $x<1$ and $y<2,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253612 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253145 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253612 1069 "q-11981" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253612 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253612 1071 "Which of the following represents the solution to the system of linear equations given below?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=4 \\\\ 2x-y=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253612 1072 #uuid "9b922ce0-59e6-5533-889a-0b37907617d2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253612 1074 "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253613 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253033 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253613 1069 "q-119816" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253613 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253613 1071 "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(x-2)^{2}(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253613 1072 #uuid "2a6ca6d7-d1ef-5811-9457-4d7cf9a54890" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253613 1074 "First, we define $f(x,y)=(x-2)^{2}(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=2$ or $y=-1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/caa61190f1a5565c58838556529715c08f9f00402c6f4b3ccb99a914e748f198.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d836463631120853e05b3b5fb9e534822cf5611747a3eb17e22333901bfb8fd3.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $x<2$ and $y>-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x<2$ and $y<-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x>2$ and $y>-1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253614 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253248 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253614 1069 "q-11984" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253614 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253614 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253614 1072 #uuid "3afe0de9-b343-5b36-b1d3-181e3380faa7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253614 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $M-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nM-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(M-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 5 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-5\\cdot 5 & =0 \\\\ 16+4\\lambda +4\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda -9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{{8}^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 9}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm 10}{2} \\\\  & =1,-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1$ and $\\lambda =-9.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253108 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253615 1069 "q-119842" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253615 1071 "Given that $y=y(t),$ what is the carrying capacity of the following logistic growth model?\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,350y-9{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=25\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253615 1072 #uuid "7499fb2b-143e-5ae9-b3d8-ef6bb5333537" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253615 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=9y(150-y),\\,y(0)=25.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=150.$ Therefore, the carrying capacity is $150.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253177 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253616 1069 "q-119846" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253616 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of dolphins in a certain ocean follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4y(7-\\frac{y}{200}),\\,y(0)=43,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible dolphin population?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253616 1072 #uuid "f968e4e5-8d70-5545-88a7-87466239a48a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253616 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4y(7-\\frac{y}{200}) \\\\  & =4y(\\frac{1\\,400-y}{200}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{200}y(1\\,400-y) \\\\  & =0.02y(1\\,400-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,400.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,400,$ and we conclude that the number of dolphins cannot exceed $1\\,400.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253182 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253617 1069 "q-119849" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253617 1071 "The population $y(t)$ of giraffes on a certain African wildlife reserve follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=17.2y-0.004{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=180,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of giraffes?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253617 1072 #uuid "819cfa7f-e175-52e2-b6a4-00c7d6cd6241" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253617 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =17.2y-0.004{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.004y(4\\,300-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=4\\,300.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $4\\,300,$ and we conclude that the number of giraffes cannot exceed $4\\,300.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252820 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253618 1069 "q-119854" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253618 1071 "The rate of growth in the number of plants $P(t)$ infected with a pest in a certain land follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.5P-0.0025{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=5.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. For which value of $P$ is the number of plants infected with the pest growing at the fastest possible rate?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253618 1072 #uuid "6038179b-b217-5d74-a035-1eee65cd6fb5" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253618 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.5P-0.0025{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.0025P(200-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=200.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $200,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 200=100.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253019 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253619 1069 "q-119855" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253619 1071 "The rate of growth in the number of turtles $P(t)$ that developed fungus on their shells in a certain aquarium follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P-0.012{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=2.\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in months. For which value of $P$ is the number of turtles that developed fungus growing at the fastest possible rate?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253619 1072 #uuid "8ba7669b-2e64-5f51-a1c7-6cb07f0a914d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253619 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](images/9d/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =0.6P-0.012{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.012P(50-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=50.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $50,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 50=25.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253140 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253620 1069 "q-119861" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253620 1071 "The rate of growth in the number of fishes $P(t)$ with a fungal infection in a certain pond is jointly proportional to the number of fishes that have been infected at time $t$ and the number that have not been infected. If the constant of proportionality is $0.009$ and the carrying capacity is $130,$ then find an expression for the number of fishes with fungal infection, given that there were $5$ fishes infected initially." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253620 1072 #uuid "9f344eaa-317e-56fb-a019-d5032dce4e04" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253620 1074 "Since the growth rate of infected fishes is *jointly* proportional to the number of fishes that have been infected at time $t,$ and the number that have not, the number of infected fishes follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.009,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=130.$ Therefore, the growth of the number of fishes with fungal infection is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.009P(130-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-(130\\cdot 0.009)t}}=\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-1.17t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=5$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{130}{1+C{e}^{-1.17\\cdot 0}} & =5 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{130}{5} \\\\ C & =26-1 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{130}{1+25{e}^{-1.17t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252841 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253621 1069 "q-119862" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253621 1071 "The growth rate of the flamingo population $P(t)$ in in a certain lagoon is jointly proportional to the flamingo population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the lagoon's carrying capacity and the flamingo population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,8$ and the carrying capacity is $16\\,000,$ then find an expression for the flamingo population after $t$ years, given that there were $2\\,000$ flamingos in the lagoon initially." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253621 1072 #uuid "87785bfa-a7cc-54e0-861c-bb86770490c2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253621 1074 "Since the flamingo population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the flamingo population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the flamingo population, the flamingo population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,8,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=16\\,000.$ Therefore, the flamingo population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(16\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(16\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,8)t}}=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-12.8t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=2\\,000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-12.8\\cdot 0}} & =2\\,000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{16\\,000}{2\\,000} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{16\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-12.8t}}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252814 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253622 1069 "q-119863" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253622 1071 "$\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253622 1072 #uuid "7b2a7a5d-5ff3-58b1-bc1e-3ba374a0cda5" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253622 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253623 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253001 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253623 1069 "q-119864" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253623 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253623 1071 "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253623 1072 #uuid "a1b437a5-869a-5ccd-aeb4-884c52b20f38" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253623 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}=-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253624 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253201 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253624 1069 "q-119866" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253624 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253624 1071 "$\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253624 1072 #uuid "8e68dcc2-8781-5eb7-a08a-4c11847234f8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253624 1074 "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)^{2}}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)^{2}}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)}{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)\\cancel{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1)}}{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253215 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253625 1069 "q-119867" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253625 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253625 1072 #uuid "af3d18fb-a784-53a2-9372-1a3bbb7ca376" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253625 1074 "First, we rewrite the numerator of the first term using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator of the first term as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253626 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252947 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253626 1069 "q-119869" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253626 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253626 1071 "If $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},$ then $f(x)=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253626 1072 #uuid "fe39df04-92b7-5f70-a2a5-5ed05c5e127c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253626 1074 "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-1}{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{2\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253627 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253239 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253627 1069 "q-119904" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253627 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253627 1071 "Simplify ${cos}^{2}⁡z\\,({cot}^{2}⁡z+1).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253627 1072 #uuid "d3d698d1-fddf-50d2-9b5a-51d1e576d1b5" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253627 1074 "Using the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡z={csc}^{2}⁡z,$ we rewrite and simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡z\\,({cot}^{2}⁡z+1) & ={cos}^{2}⁡z\\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡z \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡z\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z})}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡z\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡z} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡z}{{sin}^{2}⁡z} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z})}^{2} \\\\  & ={cot}^{2}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253628 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253147 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253628 1069 "q-119905" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253628 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253628 1071 "Simplify ${sin}^{2}⁡w\\,(1+{cot}^{2}⁡w).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253628 1072 #uuid "06466b4b-c6cd-5178-abe2-7bb6034e59d8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253628 1074 "Using the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡w={csc}^{2}⁡w,$ we rewrite and simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡w\\,(1+{cot}^{2}⁡w) & ={sin}^{2}⁡w\\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡w \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡w\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡w})}^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡w\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡w} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253629 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253072 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253629 1069 "q-119906" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253629 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253629 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta }.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253629 1072 #uuid "67f941a8-74c6-537b-aaa1-feb29dbe709f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253629 1074 "Using the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we rewrite and simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253630 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253254 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253630 1069 "q-119948" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253630 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253630 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253630 1072 #uuid "984868e6-9b0d-5753-97a4-a56b885226b2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253630 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{10}) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{10})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252965 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253631 1069 "q-120002" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253631 1071 "Samarium-147 has a half-life of $106.1$ billion years. A sample of samarium-147 has an initial mass of $300\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to the nearest integer, what is the mass of the sample after $80$ billion years?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253631 1072 #uuid "3c164d8d-6f7c-5364-9f24-9f1a44b57ac9" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253631 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=106.1$ billion years, we have\n\n$$\n106.1=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{106.1}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=300\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=300{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/106.1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(80)=300{e}^{-80\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/106.1}≈178\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that there will be $178\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ of the sample left." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253632 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252930 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253632 1069 "q-120006" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253632 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253632 1071 "The half-life of astatine-223 is $50\\,\\text{s}.$ How long does it take for an $400\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ sample of astatine-223 to reduce to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253632 1072 #uuid "e6c4bf32-422c-5447-b557-5dce8db4fbab" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253632 1074 "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}.\n$$\n\nThere are two methods that we can use to solve this problem. We describe each of them below.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=50\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n50=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{k}\\,⇒\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{50}\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=400\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=400{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ So, we set up our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n50=400{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =400{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50} \\\\ \\frac{50}{400} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{8}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2/50}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{50} \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡8 & =-\\frac{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{50} \\\\ t & =\\frac{50\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ t & =150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $150\\,\\text{s}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe wish to calculate the time $t$ such that $M(t)=50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{M(t)}{M(0)} & =\\frac{50\\,\\mu \\text{g}}{400\\,\\mu \\text{g}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\nM(t)={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}\\cdot M(0).\n$$\n\nFrom this equation, we see that $3$ half-lives must have occurred for the mass to reduce from $400\\,\\mu \\text{g}$ to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\nt=3\\cdot {t}_{1/2}=3\\cdot 50\\,\\text{s}=150\\,\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that it takes $150\\,\\text{s}$ for the mass of the substance to reduce to $50\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253170 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253633 1069 "q-120017" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253633 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253633 1072 #uuid "1a84df43-c63e-5997-bb62-0b42cedd5faa" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253633 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nRewriting the given expression using the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253634 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253050 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253634 1069 "q-120018" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253634 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253634 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253634 1072 #uuid "11defe63-9b06-5fba-87b6-5807bf8b34d3" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253634 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253635 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253185 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253635 1069 "q-120020" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253635 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253635 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253635 1072 #uuid "83d0963d-a8db-5840-a576-ccf3c21b5f89" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253635 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ in the numerator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253120 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253636 1069 "q-120024" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253636 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253636 1072 #uuid "ceef643d-5955-5dc4-9610-8fc14edd1b2d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253636 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})$ in the numerator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253637 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252923 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253637 1069 "q-120025" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253637 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253637 1071 "![](images/17/17cd72acbe8dd412d37c51ff992278d9664f6c5b1141a8604278fda76e57700f.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x-2$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253637 1072 #uuid "bf3614e8-6b2e-5b66-a1c3-8c10682d44be" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253637 1074 "![](images/5d/5d6165488c8e41c16cf31b1b000a924344399c2b94a8fe5e7138c55adf97602b.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x-2$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not evaluate to zero at both $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x-2)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=x(x+1)(x-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $x=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $x=-1,$ which is not a behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253144 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253638 1069 "q-120029" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253638 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{100}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}}{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253638 1072 #uuid "df53af64-a7fb-5920-a9ae-4765f5d7236c" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253638 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={50}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {50}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({100}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{100}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}}{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}}}{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sin}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}}{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing  the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}=1-{cos}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} & =\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}}} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253098 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253639 1069 "q-120030" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253639 1071 "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{140}^{∘}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{70}^{∘}}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}}=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253639 1072 #uuid "20492e7a-2ad5-5346-9935-34abf42dcea6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253639 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={70}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {70}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{140}^{∘}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{70}^{∘}}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}}}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡{70}^{∘}}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing  the Pythagorean identity in the form ${cos}^{2}⁡{70}^{∘}=1-{sin}^{2}⁡{70}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡{70}^{∘}}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2(1-{sin}^{2}⁡{70}^{∘})}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘})(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘})}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘})\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘})}}{\\cancel{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}}} \\\\  & =2(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252878 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253640 1069 "q-120032" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253640 1071 "Find the exact value of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘}).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253640 1072 #uuid "2a04b487-4a85-5ba7-af3b-ed0dfdddcb78" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253640 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={105}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘}) & =4(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘})) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({105}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({105}^{∘})=-2.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252818 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253641 1069 "q-120038" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253641 1071 "![](images/f8/f855062048a7d38b2fa6c843701a182661b2235b2042ad83fbedd600b1dd770c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{10}(x+3)(x-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{5}(x+3)(2-x)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-3)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253641 1072 #uuid "a07799d5-cf00-5be4-9950-028a4e658d3e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253641 1074 "![](images/8f/8fdec9bda9e2bcc514e56a14a6608b2f0cb74fd0943a51c944b29cc047e02cd9.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{10}(x+3)(x-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{5}(x+3)(2-x)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(x+2)(x-3)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253029 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253642 1069 "q-120058" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253642 1071 "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253642 1072 #uuid "ad1365bd-d309-5764-8412-78c271945c47" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253642 1074 "First, we note that:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253207 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253643 1069 "q-120070" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253643 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253643 1071 "![](images/51/51273365c4a282dc3dedd9d7cd5ac7d805e1526aaeee1915e8bd95d8223c26d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253643 1072 #uuid "b69e6ff7-5b7e-5a36-bad5-97913bd7c4d8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253643 1074 "![](images/a1/a14651ffc883a8c24ad17bdd031658c3d4ae21db2cfded213f2ba911fdb9a765.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-3$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+3x=x(x+3)\n$$\n\ngives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-1$ and $x=0.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+3)\n$$\n\ndoes *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $x<-3$ | $-3<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252918 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253644 1069 "q-120072" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253644 1071 "![](images/6e/6e57e25ea43f23e9d157b3fa1c621749d3d1a88bab389af66449c915d9e03606.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253644 1072 #uuid "0feec3dd-4e22-5c34-8479-7f71c6edf6bb" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253644 1074 "![](images/52/523f18dc7b081ecf6dcfa1f09c6fe715012fffba3e4e07d1dfd44c3d53cf702c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1),\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+x=x(x+1).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=-1$ and $x=0$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-1$ | $-1<x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}={x}^{2}(x+1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252825 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253645 1069 "q-120073" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253645 1071 "![](images/11/1135d4a7b28f5cd1ab046f89e2882cd9074b43f95b2076b261e04003e4330c14.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253645 1072 #uuid "e76de391-3286-5c0a-8a10-3ff8adb22ee8" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253645 1074 "![](images/f6/f68f830dd6d0e6fb205bfa1ac56b1818b676089a12a5bcab46699210ba9e3191.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-4x=x(x+2)(x-2),\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}={x}^{2}(2+x)(2-x).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 2$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $x<-2$ | $-2<x<0$ | $0<x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-4x=x(x+2)(x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}={x}^{3}-4x.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253111 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253646 1069 "q-120076" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253646 1071 "![](images/5f/5fdb2ddd3d07537c1e3d0d22d435928f7cd11dc7a71c67f1bf45292c82a61ef7.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253646 1072 #uuid "c84723b8-0d5e-5a26-bf52-a830d4f235da" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253646 1074 "![](images/cf/cf2ba44d8cc21ade2204a3784af7d09b492843339a8b98c4cde741b30dfa54db.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The solution curves have the vertical asymptote $x=-1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(x+1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side does not approach $\\pm \\infty $ as $x\\to -1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x+1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not approach $\\infty $ as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}=\\frac{x}{(1+\\frac{1}{x})}\\to \\pm \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation become vertical as $x\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253020 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253647 1069 "q-120081" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253647 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $({cot}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}-2{csc}^{2}⁡x+1?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253647 1072 #uuid "5c9d3edb-7e85-5f62-9625-703bb7592849" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253647 1074 "First, we recall the identities\n\n$$\n{cot}^{2}⁡x+1={csc}^{2}⁡x,\\,{csc}^{2}⁡x-1={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThen, we simplify our expression using the above identities, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({cot}^{2}⁡x+1)^{2}-2{csc}^{2}⁡x+1 & =({csc}^{2}⁡x)^{2}-2\\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =({csc}^{2}⁡x-1)^{2} \\\\  & =({cot}^{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={cot}^{4}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252910 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253648 1069 "q-120088" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253648 1071 "$4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}⁡({140}^{∘})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253648 1072 #uuid "8280d3f1-0c7e-5f24-8bb1-95c62d7445a7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253648 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={70}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {70}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{csc}⁡({140}^{∘}) & =2\\cdot [2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({70}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}⁡({70}^{∘})]\\operatorname{csc}⁡({140}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({140}^{∘})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253218 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253649 1069 "q-120093" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253649 1071 "$8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253649 1072 #uuid "91b3ae38-a411-55e5-ba0c-b0976c1d82dd" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253649 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{32}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{32})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) & =4[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{32})]\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16},$ we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) & =2[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{16})] \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})] \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253018 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253650 1069 "q-120095" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253650 1071 "![](images/a7/a7f100b99cdd9ff4f19209be8df1ae01d4cf1254d67f985cd6de41aefbf51e31.png)\n\nJill unfolds a cardboard box and obtains a net shown above. What is the total surface area of the box?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253650 1072 #uuid "e4e17473-1ee5-55b2-88f1-975d59c79f16" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253650 1074 "The net shown corresponds to a rectangular prism. The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ squares (base and top) and $4$ rectangles (side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+4\\times (\\text{Area of the side}) \\\\  & =2\\times 360+4\\times 420 \\\\  & =720+1\\,680 \\\\  & =2\\,400\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253214 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253651 1069 "q-120106" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253651 1071 "![](images/76/76a0135eba2c33a3e69f9893d7c32a4cdc93e6d99825bbf6f72fb1db72b2a590.png)\n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by ${y}^{′}=(y-a)(y-b)^{2}.$ Find the value of $ab.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253651 1072 #uuid "f543304f-c119-5b55-a8a8-833fe3a8d3f1" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253651 1074 "![](images/68/68f424470a3a8ff98d7f738ce207c240795185e48ee527a434d04282d9023ed7.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=1$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y-1)(y-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1,b=4,$ and $ab=4.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252876 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253652 1069 "q-120109" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253652 1071 "![](images/3d/3d24906da8cd181d40dbaec0095b8d51481babc152609fc378b03c61187220c3.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253652 1072 #uuid "c0faaaf1-6fed-5e12-af7b-9fca9c443b1e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253652 1074 "![](images/7f/7f8a042b9944b2cdf0e864b7dca760f094f4413f197befa05d6e5cbefb3841ba.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)(y-2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2,$ but it also evaluates to zero when $y=1,$ which is not behavior exhibited by our slope field.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=\\pm 1.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}(2-y)$ gives the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253252 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253653 1069 "q-12011" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253653 1071 "Find the standard matrix that represents  the  linear  transformation  $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}y-3x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253653 1072 #uuid "f5d2b89d-649f-58f6-8911-6e694e8c52ce" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253653 1074 "First, rewrite the order of the variables in the usual way:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-3x+y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3x+y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3x+y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation  $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253250 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253654 1069 "q-12012" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253654 1071 "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} \\\\ 7y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is **NOT** a linear transformation" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253654 1072 #uuid "a3a19c98-b506-54b2-aa6b-86aada819468" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253654 1074 "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}(kx)^{2} \\\\ 7(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}({x}^{2}) \\\\ k(7y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}k({x}^{2}) \\\\ 7y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is not a linear transformation.\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})^{2} \\\\ 7({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+7{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2} \\\\ 7{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 7{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253132 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253655 1069 "q-120147" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253655 1071 "![](images/d2/d27af23ca398eba62c2ab1fe2ee2cf9bfdf736c16fdd8408d2a13e0d34dae32c.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253655 1072 #uuid "efa92615-fa1f-50ea-a45f-eb618f2a603d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253655 1074 "![](images/ab/ab076b61b443e031455f07c1e976467cc3b74651bfb5fc2fb39f89a74e156681.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}(2-y)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=0$ and $y=2,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y-2)^{2}$ gives the required behavior.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=y(y+2)$ does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side evaluates to zero at $y=-2$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253231 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253656 1069 "q-120150" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253656 1071 "![](images/8d/8d2588ba2b8987841a34e981aa6b7d48cdc41bebfca6c0517cc9cf67d788f591.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253656 1072 #uuid "78ee0f36-5161-5385-a32f-abf2242167bd" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253656 1074 "![](images/1b/1b5303303f0aea22149b3d418e08724cfea9cf64943eab978fe38c0d025d73bf.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $y=\\pm 1.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should equal zero on both of these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-1)^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)(y-1) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =(y+1)^{2}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 1$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-1$ | $-1<y<1$ | $y>1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=(y+1)(y-1)^{2}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253081 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253657 1069 "q-120159" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253657 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=2?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253657 1072 #uuid "701345d5-a1b3-50a0-9826-bd376abc5cd0" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253657 1074 "The polar curve defined by the equation $r=2$ is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin. Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/73/739d610bf181443a1c0b5c8103fcb0af89d8b1b40ecf7f6e83837f7c326697ed.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252929 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253658 1069 "q-120160" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253658 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=1?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253658 1072 #uuid "fca1dbe3-488f-54c4-89e7-ec6b809c4d52" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253658 1074 "The polar curve defined by the equation $r=1$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/db/db4f4edb33bf4f4f93d62b66f1dc0caa7a9d37d1441f070c277e28e9b64233e9.png)" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253235 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253659 1069 "q-120166" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253659 1071 "Find the Cartesian coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=7$ intersects the positive $y$-axis." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253659 1072 #uuid "7e0e2eab-e496-5a93-be3e-709e1622b8f5" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253659 1074 "The polar curve $r=7$ is a circle of radius $7$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $y$-axis at the point $(0,7),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/cf/cf8bdc8c72bbc86361dd9378575d8daa10d775132c909b88a77ee185e57fe116.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,7).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253232 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253660 1069 "q-120169" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253660 1071 "Find the Cartesian coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=3$ intersects the negative $x$-axis." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253660 1072 #uuid "c538a2a9-f441-50c2-b2b2-9978dbea640e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253660 1074 "The polar curve $r=3$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $x$-axis at the point $(-3,0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/19/19c6d04de928de62e63fb949cfcd770d130f8836a0bf0da06d90e7d3cb5915b2.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-3,0).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253222 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253661 1069 "q-120171" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253661 1071 "Find the **polar** coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=4$ intersects the negative $y$-axis." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253661 1072 #uuid "5d0dc5da-5a41-5ef8-908b-57e6af4cb686" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253661 1074 "The polar curve $r=4$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $y$-axis at the point where $r=4$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/08/0861ee15a875e00738a237d2788e26432c1c4c128ad7510a881f31aebaaff491.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252898 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253662 1069 "q-120173" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253662 1071 "Find the **polar** coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=3$ intersects the positive $x$-axis." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253662 1072 #uuid "ad9bd804-bb85-5c6d-b744-72eae38d2456" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253662 1074 "The polar curve $r=3$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It crosses the positive $x$-axis at the point where $r=3$ and $\\theta =0.$\n\n![](images/63/63d6e0d36e3d345561b1c4c20d7d19cb34ae6bd3b7543c042be288d8fcdb12e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection in polar coordinates is $(3,0).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253166 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253663 1069 "q-120174" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253663 1071 "![](images/e0/e0da4ce40e23a44ddfd946b9a9bf00882ca0a83db8379c6492b58355772c6006.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253663 1072 #uuid "38710c32-eda6-56be-a71f-8477ad447bb6" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253663 1074 "![](images/dd/ddac2bab6fb72979e1142179dfb11792cc50044de545131139edfdeff9215e3c.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=\\pm 2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-4=(y+2)(y-2).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=\\pm 2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<-2$ | $-2<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}=(2+y)(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=4-{y}^{2}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253126 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253664 1069 "q-120176" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253664 1071 "![](images/44/440d321dd0eb1b5687b392aac95d5d7d36e5a31d4cb95d5397f88afe229d7aa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253664 1072 #uuid "0927bb0b-0c34-59cb-9a44-279569caf3e2" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253664 1074 "![](images/0a/0a8b34730f001c218b6c380b9f5af0ff4b2ffd8fbb295f82e4961c5242051986.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-2y=y(y-2)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↗$ | $↗$ | $↘$ |\n| Sign | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}={y}^{2}(2-y).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252974 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253665 1069 "q-120177" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253665 1071 "![](images/8d/8d004b698e288cbfa3cab67b1744bd9bf40c018267fa1f44a633019f6f047a9a.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253665 1072 #uuid "9d412471-73d3-5230-8ea1-8e43c7be125b" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253665 1074 "![](images/89/895353a5359d43f8f6256453d4f97a4f35b33acd1241f0a2950b9b21f6313f50.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope field is zero along the lines $y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines.\n\nOf the given equations, the only two that satisfy this condition are\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}=2{y}^{2}(y-2)\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-{y}^{4}={y}^{3}(2-y).\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $y=0$ and $y=2$ divide the plane into $3$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $3$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| Region | $y<0$ | $0<y<2$ | $y>2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Slope | $↘$ | $↘$ | $↗$ |\n| Sign | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nOf the two remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}=2{y}^{2}(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=2{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253114 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253666 1069 "q-12018" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253666 1071 "$\\int \\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253666 1072 #uuid "03a455ba-2873-5368-831e-308d6a1a251d" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253666 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=2x-3.$Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6(2x-3)^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{u}^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {u}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3}){u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-{u}^{-3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}+C] & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[-\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{3}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(2x-3)^{-3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =-(-3)\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x-3)+0 \\\\  & =3\\cdot (2x-3)^{-4}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{(2x-3)^{4}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253105 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253667 1069 "q-120181" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253667 1071 "![](images/c3/c3d18d92739af7c6ea7c2a8560ab54c4c08b8b20f9ee33ba52a254b16315d9bf.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(9,12)$, as shown above." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253667 1072 #uuid "85dff27c-cd0d-5431-91dd-d638f2703c2e" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253667 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225} \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=15.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253054 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253668 1069 "q-120186" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253668 1071 "![](images/f8/f8b8c044d79ce592089365b3918dfae0afc52ef86a71c899abe823c828ab23b0.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(1,-1)$, as shown above." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253668 1072 #uuid "b3691fe4-f7f9-5f4e-89c5-6571f374e002" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253668 1074 "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253045 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253669 1069 "q-120196" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253669 1071 "![](images/1a/1a3ba94aa37bd8b8494794a7013956b100f418c9e0aa7efecab5ba6fc6476ff6.png)\n\nIdentify the differential equation that generated the slope field above." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253669 1072 #uuid "35c50a43-bdb2-567c-9f1b-ac02b598aef3" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253669 1074 "![](images/1a/1a3ba94aa37bd8b8494794a7013956b100f418c9e0aa7efecab5ba6fc6476ff6.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to \\pm 1.$\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(y+1)(y-1)} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{(1+y)(1-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the slopes become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}\\to 0\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}$ become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nOn the other hand,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}$ do *not* become horizontal as $y\\to 0.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-1}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253234 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253670 1069 "q-120228" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253670 1071 "![](images/20/204be6af825ef678e06526bff1fdac14ca5f09a2a7bee939620f3f2be1496985.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x+3)y$\n- ${y}^{′}=(3-x)y$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253670 1072 #uuid "f78fc747-0bca-532e-9197-bd4cfa23233a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253670 1074 "![](images/90/9051213882bcb506ddae46e0a6a79c8cd03a10eadd89e0007f895f68b00c33de.png)\n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=3$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on these lines. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each equation:\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x+3)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=-3$ and $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(3-x)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=3$ and $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253208 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253671 1069 "q-120232" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253671 1071 "![](images/be/be758c93cb1e04380de9b57103c302623400342946996030b22ae12046488172.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$\n- ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(y-3)$\n- ${y}^{′}=x(y-3)^{2}$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253671 1072 #uuid "9d55cc1b-b5c1-5142-aa9a-80b5b9474cd7" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253671 1074 "![](images/3a/3afe29e9dd507cd9aae4035ef3bde2f3f2c1865cd258c4d218ec1934d8626a6e.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=3,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=3$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>3$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(3-y)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}(y-3)$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(y-3)^{2}$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=3,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253164 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253672 1069 "q-120234" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253672 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253672 1072 #uuid "0eb4fa3e-52f2-52cc-a5b3-7f2b007d645a" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253672 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252851 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253673 1069 "q-120235" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253673 1071 "![](images/19/19f3d391ff30338ae70f59f1cf1ad40922eb8146c2be0993253a99173db2925a.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x(y-2)^{2}$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-2)^{2}y$\n- ${y}^{′}=(x-2)y$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253673 1072 #uuid "e5c81757-4f06-5306-9513-674ada3bee66" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253673 1074 "![](images/e3/e337a67c316dbee9e9c73849f363a7b2c3e3cb485bfef8eb49e87c3e33b98cf1.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=2$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines.\n\n- Second, the lines $x=2$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x(y-2)^{2}$ evaluates to zero at $x=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x-2)^{2}y$ evaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ but the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) do not correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=(x-2)y$ evaluates to zero at $x=2$ and $y=0,$ and the sign of the slopes in the required regions (summarized below) correspond to the given slope field.\n\n| $y>0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186253127 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253674 1069 "q-120241" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253674 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-2a,a),$ where $a$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$  Round your answer to the nearest tenth where appropriate." 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253674 1072 #uuid "0c3da906-9678-5457-978c-c32d8842e209" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253674 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2a)^{2}+{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{-2a})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})| \\\\  & ≈0.463\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ≈\\pi -0.463\\,6 \\\\  & ≈2.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(\\sqrt{5}\\text{ }\\,a,2.7).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186252988 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253675 1069 "q-120243" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253675 1071 "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-a,a),$ where $a$ is a positive real number. What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253675 1072 #uuid "1855c488-31cc-5b55-93ba-bcc9b609f94f" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true] [17592186253675 1074 "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-a)^{2}+{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{a}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{-a})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }\\,a,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139533396 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0008821a-eb35-5a25-b9b1-0c2a29881e9a" 17592186250558 "answer-00677872-7616-5c6b-bc06-e85a41bca0b0" 17592186250559 "answer-0070aff9-6410-5cd7-9aaf-a51985939ad0" 17592186250560 "answer-007e5875-12bb-5469-bee2-9e308d1913ac" 17592186250561 "answer-00a25b06-d824-5579-bf60-2d2c4e45d9a5" 17592186250562 "answer-00a984ec-000a-5987-a4ce-bb056594af64" 17592186250563 "answer-00eabfa3-cbd8-5d06-b474-2130cea60e32" 17592186250564 "answer-0107d14e-cab2-5d42-b4d4-e9ca633982f4" 17592186250